\h 2 Kings
\toc1 The second summary of the Kings of Israel and Judah
\toc2 2 Kings
\mt2 The second summary of the
\mt1 Kings of Israel and Judah
\rem This is still an early look into the unpolished text of the Open English Translation of the Bible. Please double-check the text in advance before using in public.
\c 1
\s1 Yahweh punishes King Ahazyah
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 After the death of \add Israel’s\add* \add King\add* Ahab, Moab rebelled against Israel.
\p
\v 2 \add One day, King\add* Ahazyah fell through a lattice in his upper room in Samaria and was injured, so he sent messengers to go and ask Ekron’s god Baal-Zevuv if he would recover.
\v 3 But Yahweh’s messenger told Elijah from Tishbeh), “Leave here \add and\add* go and meet the Samaria king’s messengers and ask them, ‘Is it because there’s no god in Israel that you’re all going to inquire from Ekron’s god Baal-Zevuv?
\v 4 Because of that, Yahweh says that \add the king\add* won’t get out of the bed that he’s lying in—he’s definitely going to die.”
\p So Elijah \add told them and\add* continued on his way.
\v 5 When the messengers returned to the king, he asked them, “What’s this? Why are you back here \add so soon\add*?”
\p
\v 6 “A man came to meet us,” they told him, “and he said to us, ‘Go \add and\add* return to the king who sent you, and tell him that Yahweh says this: Is it because there’s no god in Israel that you are sending messengers to inquire from Ekron’s god Baal-Zevuv? Therefore you’ll never leave that bed that you’re lying in but you’ll certainly die \add in it\add*.’ ”
\p
\v 7 “What was the man like,” the king asked them, “who met you and gave you all that message?”
\p
\v 8 “He had a cloak made of animal hair,” they answered, “and was wearing a leather belt.”
\p “\add Ah,\add*” said the king. “That’s Elijah from Tishbeh.”
\p
\v 9 So he sent a captain with his fifty soldiers to look for Elijah and they found him sitting on the top of a hill. The captain called out, “Man of God, the king wants to see you.”
\p
\v 10 But Elijah replied, “Well, if I am a man of God, let fire come down from the sky and consume you and your men,” and fire came down from the sky, and it burnt up the captain and his men.
\p
\v 11 So the king sent another captain with his fifty men and they went to where Elijah was and called out to him, “Prophet, the king commands that you come with us right now.”
\p
\v 12 \add Again\add* Elijah replied, “If I am a man of God, let fire come down from the sky and consume you and your men,” and God’s fire came down from the sky, and it burnt up the captain and his men.
\p
\v 13 So the king sent a third captain with his fifty men. They went to where Elijah was and the officer knelt down in front of him and pleaded, “Prophet, I beg you, be kind to me and my fifty soldiers, and don’t kill us.
\v 14 Listen, fire came down from the sky and burnt up the first two captains and their men, but please treat my life as valuable in your sight.”
\p
\v 15 Then Yahweh’s messenger told Elijah, “Go with them. Don’t be afraid of him.”
\p So Elijah went with them to the king
\v 16 and told him, “Yahweh says that you sent messengers to inquire from Ekron’s god Baal-Zevuv, as if there’s no god in Israel to ask. Therefore, \add he says,\add* you certainly won’t get off that bed that you’re on because you’ll die \add in it\add*.”
\p
\v 17 So King Ahazyah died just as Yahweh had said via Elijah. Then \add Ahazyah’s younger brother\add* Jehoram became king in his place, because Ahazyah didn’t have any sons. \add That happened\add* in the second year \add of the reign\add* of Jehoshaphat’s son King Jehoram over Judah.
\v 18 Everything else that Ahazyah said and did is written in the book of the events of the kings of Israel.
\c 2
\s1 Elijah is taken up to heaven
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 One day as Elijah and Elisha had left Gilgal, Yahweh took Elijah up to heaven in a storm.
\v 2 Elijah had told Elisha, “Now, you stay here because Yahweh has told me to go to Bethel.”
\p “By the life of Yahweh,” Elisha had responded, “and by the life of your spirit, I won’t leave you.” So they’d headed to Bethel.
\p
\v 3 Also the prophets-in-training who were in Bethel had gone out to Elisha and told him, “Do you know that today Yahweh is going to take your master away from you?”
\p “I already know that,” he’d replied. “Don’t say anything.”
\p
\v 4 Then Elijah had informed Elisha, “Yahweh has sent me to Jericho. You stay here.”
\p He’d answered, “By the life of Yahweh and the life of your spirit, I won’t leave you.” So they’d gone to Jericho.
\p
\v 5 The prophets-in-training who were in Jericho had approached Elisha and briefed him, “Do you know that today Yahweh is taking your master away from you?”
\p “Yes, I realise that,” he’d responded. “Say no more.”
\p
\v 6 Again Elijah had told him, “You stay here, because Yahweh has sent me to the Jordan.”
\p And he’d countered, “By the life of Yahweh and the life of your spirit, I won’t leave you.” So the two of them had gone there together.
\v 7 Fifty of the prophets-in-training had also gone, but they’d stood back watching from a distance, so the two of them had stood there by the Jordan.
\v 8 Then Elijah had taken off his cloak and doubled it over and hit the river \add with it\add*. The water divided leaving a dry path and the two of them had walked across \add to the other side\add*.
\v 9 As they’d crossed over, Elijah had told Elisha, “Tell me what you’d like me to do for you before I get taken away.”
\p “Please,” Elisha had answered, “I’d like a double amount of the spirit that you have.”
\p
\v 10 “\add Oh,\add* that’s a difficult request,” Elijah had responded. “\add Well,\add* if you see me being taken away from you, then you’ll get what you asked for, but if you miss seeing me go, you won’t.”
\p
\v 11 Then it had happened \add while\add* they were walking and talking \add together\add*. Wow, a chariot of fire and horses of fire \add drove\add* between the two of them, separating them. Then Elijah went up to the heavens in the gale.
\v 12 Elisha was watching, and he cried out, “My father, my father, Israel’s chariot and its horsemen!”
\p But then Elisha could no longer see him, and he grabbed his \add own\add* cloak, and tore it in half,
\v 13 and he picked up Elijah’s cloak that had fallen down to the ground. Then he went back and stood on the bank of the Jordan,
\v 14 and he took Elijah’s cloak and hit the river \add with it\add*, saying, “Where is Elijah’s god Yahweh?” Then as he hit the water, the river divided into two parts and Elisha crossed through the middle.
\v 15 The prophets-in-training who were on the Jericho side, affirmed, “Elisha now has the spirit that Elijah had.” Then they came \add forward\add* to meet him and knelt down in front of him \add as a sign of respect\add*,
\v 16 and they requested, “Listen please, fifty of us here have military training. Please, let them go to search for your master, in case Yahweh’s spirit lifted him and dropped him on a hill somewhere or into one of the valleys.”
\p “Don’t waste your time,” he replied.
\v 17 But they kept insisting so much that Elisha was embarrassed and he said, “Ok then.”
\p So they sent fifty men, and they searched for three days, but they didn’t find Elijah.
\v 18 When they returned to Elisha (he was staying in Jericho), he said to them, “Didn’t I tell you not to bother?”
\p
\v 19 Then the leaders of \add Jericho\add* city said to Elisha, “Please, this city is a good place to live, just as my master can see, but the water \add supplies around here\add* are bad so our crops don’t thrive.”
\p
\v 20 “Bring me a \add brand\add* new bowl,” he said, “and put salt there.” So they brought one to him,
\v 21 and he went out to the spring and threw the salt at it, and announced, “Yahweh says that he’s healed these water \add supplies\add* and they’ll no longer cause death or barrenness.”
\v 22 So now those water supplies have been healthy and beneficial until this day, just as Elisha had said.
\p
\v 23 From there Elisha went to Bethel, and as he was going up on the road, \add some\add* young men came out from the city, and started mocking him, saying, “Keep moving, baldy. Keep moving, baldy.”
\p
\v 24 Then Elisha turned back around and looked at them, and he cursed them in Yahweh’s name. Just then, two female bears came out from the forest and mauled forty-two of them.
\p
\v 25 Elisha carried on from there to Mt. Carmel, and from there, he returned to Samaria.
\c 3
\s1 The war between Israel and Moab
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Ahab’s son Jehoram became king over Israel in the eighteenth year of King Jehoshaphat’s \add reign over\add* Judah, and he reigned from Samaria for twelve years.
\v 2 He did what Yahweh had said was evil, but not as bad as his father and mother \add had been\add*, and he did remove the pillar of Baal that his father had made.
\v 3 However, he committed similar sins to those of Nevat’s son Jeroboam who had caused Israel to sin and didn’t stop doing them.
\v 4 Now Moab’s King Meysha was a sheep breeder, and he would pay an annual tribute to Israel’s king of a hundred thousand young rams, plus the wool of a hundred thousand rams.
\v 5 However, after King Ahab died, Moab’s king rebelled against Israel’s control over them.
\v 6 So King Jehoram set out from Samaria and mustered all of Israel’s fighters.
\v 7 Then he sent this message to King Jehoshaphat of Judah, “Moab’s king has rebelled against me. Will you join in battle with me against them?”
\p “I’ll join you,” he replied. “You and me are together. My people are your people, and my horses are your horses.
\v 8 What’s our strategy? Which way should we go in?” he asked.
\p “Through the Edom wilderness,” he replied.
\p
\v 9 So the kings of Israel, Judah, and Edom \add took\add* their \add armies\add* on a seven day trip \add circling around\add*, but they didn’t find enough water for the men or their animals,
\v 10 and Israel’s king complained, “Oh \add dear\add*, it seems that Yahweh has sent in us three kings to allow Moab to defeat us.”
\v 11 But Jehoshaphat asked, “Isn’t there one of Yahweh’s prophets here, so we might inquire from Yahweh through him?”
\p “There’s Elisha,” one of the servants of Israel’s king answered, “Shafat’s son, \add the prophet\add* who was Elijah’s assistant.”
\p
\v 12 “\add Yes,\add* he receives Yahweh’s messages,” said Jehoshaphat.
\p So the kings of Israel, Judah, and Edom, went to see him.
\v 13 But Elisha asked Israel’s king, “What do you and me have in common? Go \add and inquire from\add* your father’s prophets and your mother’s prophets.”
\p “No,” the king of Israel said. “\add We came\add* because Yahweh has called for us three kings to allow us to be defeated by Moab.”
\p
\v 14 “As Commander-in-chief Yahweh lives,” Elisha responded, “the one who I serve, I wouldn’t be talking to you or even face you, if I hadn’t recognised King Jehoshaphat of Judah.
\v 15 So now bring me a musician.
\p Then while the musician played, Yahweh spoke to him
\v 16 and he said, “Yahweh says that this valley will store lots of water.\f + \fr 3:16 \ft It’s not really clear if he’s saying that Yahweh will fill natural pools in the valley with water, or if he’s instructing the armies to dig trenches or scrape pools that Yahweh will then fill with water.\f*
\v 17 He says that you all won’t see any wind or rain, yet this valley will be filled with water, and you and your animals \add will have plenty\add* to drink.
\v 18 That’s a piece of cake for Yahweh. What’s more, he’ll give you victory over Moab.
\v 19 You’ll all attack every important city, including their fortified ones. You can chop down their fruit trees, block up their springs, and ruin their good land with stones.”
\p
\v 20 The next day at the time of the morning sacrifice, to their amazement there was water flowing from Edom and covering the ground.
\p
\v 21 \add By then\add*, the people of Moab had heard that the kings had come to battle them, so they summoned everyone who could wear a sword and stationed them beside the border.
\v 22 But when they got up early the next morning and the sun rose, from the direction where they were, the water appeared to be red like blood.
\v 23 “That’s blood,” they exclaimed. “Those \add three\add* kings must have turned against each other and killed each other. So let’s go, Moab, and collect the spoil.”
\p
\v 24 But when they reached the allied camp, the allies attacked and they fled away. Then the allies entered Moab and attacked there.
\v 25 They tore down the cities, and each person threw a stone onto the good land, making it useless. They blocked up every spring, and they chopped down every good tree. \add Eventually,\add* only \add their capital city of\add* Kir-Hareset remained standing with its stone walls, but they attacked that as well using slingshots.
\p
\v 26 When Moab’s king saw that the attackers were stronger than them, he took a team of seven hundred swordsmen to try to break through to the king of Edom, but they failed.
\v 27 So he took his oldest son (who would have become the next king) and sacrificed him \add publicly on top of the city\add* wall. That made their people crazy furious against Israel, so they left and returned home.
\c 4
\s1 Elisha helps a poor widow
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add One day\add* a woman who was a wife of one of the prophets-in-training, called out to Elisha, “Your servant, my husband, died, and you yourself know that your servant served Yahweh. Now the moneylender has come to take two of my children in payment to become his slaves.”
\p
\v 2 “\add Hmmh,\add* what could I do to help you?” Elisha responded. “Tell me, what have you got in your house?”
\p “Your servant’s got nothing in the house except a flask of oil,” she replied.
\p
\v 3 He told her, “Go \add and\add* ask for empty containers from all of your neighbours. Make sure you get plenty of them.
\v 4 Then go and shut the door behind you and your sons, and pour oil into all those containers. Once each one’s full, set it aside.”
\v 5 So she went and she shut the door \add of her house\add* with her and her sons inside. They would bring \add the empty containers\add* to her, and she poured \add the oil\add*.
\v 6 Eventually when she told one of her sons to bring the next container, he told her that there wasn’t any more, and at that point, the oil stopped flowing.
\p
\v 7 Then she went and informed the man of God, and he told her, “Go \add and\add* sell the oil and pay off your debt. Then you and your sons can live on the rest.”
\s1 Elisha brings a child back to life
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 8 One day, Elisha went to Shunem, and there was a wealthy woman there who invited him in for a meal. After that, he dropped in there for a meal whenever he was going past,
\v 9 and the woman said to her husband, “You know, I’m sure that this man who often drops in on us is a holy man of God.
\v 10 Please, can we make a small upstairs room on our flat roof? And we could put a bed and a lampstand in it for him, plus a table and chair. Then whenever he’s passing through here, he’ll have his own spot to stay.”
\p
\v 11 Another day when Elisha arrived, he went up to his room and lay down.
\v 12 Then he told his servant Gehazi to call the Shunamit \add woman\add*, so he called her and she \add came and\add* stood in front of Elisha.
\v 13 He told Gehazi, “Now tell her that we’ve noticed how she looks after us, and ask her what we can do for her? Can we speak to the king for her or to the captain of the army?”
\p But she replied, “I have family and friends all around me.”
\p
\v 14 \add After she’d left again,\add* he asked \add Gehazi\add*, “\add Well\add*, what can I do for her?”
\p “\add Hmmh,\add*” Gehazi replied. “She doesn’t have a son, and her husband is old.”
\p
\v 15 “Call her then,” Elisha said. So he called her and she came and stood in the doorway,
\v 16 and Elisha told her, “In due course after the normal months, you’ll have a son to hold.”
\p “No, my master, man of God,” she stammered. “Don’t lie to your female servant.”
\p
\v 17 Then the woman became pregnant and gave birth to a son after the normal time, just as Elisha had told her.
\p
\v 18 The child grew up and one day he went out to his father who was with the harvesters,
\v 19 and he called to his father, “My head, my head.”
\p His father told one of the servants, “Carry him \add home\add* to his mother.”
\p
\v 20 So the servant carried him back to his mother, and he sat on her knees until noon when he died.
\v 21 His mother carried him up and laid him on the bed of the man of God, shutting the door behind her as she went out.
\v 22 Then she called her husband and told him, “Please send one of the young men and one of the donkeys to me so I can run and see the man of God and then come back.”
\v 23 “Why are you going there today?” he asked. “It’s not a religious holiday.”
\p “It’ll be okay,” she answered,
\v 24 and she saddled the donkey, and told the young man, “\add Now, let’s\add* go! You lead and don’t slacken off for my comfort unless I tell you to.”
\v 25 So they set off to seek out the man of God at Mt. Carmel.
\p When Elisha saw her \add coming\add* from a distance, he told Gehazi, “Look, there’s the Shunamit woman.
\v 26 Now, please, run to meet her and ask her, ‘Are you all right? Is your husband all right? Is your child all right?’ ”
\p “Everything’s all right,” she answered.
\v 27 Then she got to the mountain and to the man of God, and she \add knelt and\add* grabbed his feet. Gehazi came over to push her away, but the man of God said, “Leave her because she’s upset, but Yahweh has hidden the problem from me—he hasn’t informed me.”
\p
\v 28 “Did I ask for a son from \add you\add*, my master?” she said. “\add No, but\add* didn’t I say, ‘Don’t mislead me?’ ”
\v 29 Then he told Gehazi, “Tuck in your robe and take my staff in your hand, and go \add quickly\add*. If you meet anyone, \add just keep going and don’t stop to\add* bless them, and if someone blesses you, \add don’t stop to\add* answer them. \add When you get there,\add* lay my staff on the young man’s face.”
\p
\v 30 But the mother of the young man said, “By the life of Yahweh and the life of your spirit, I won’t leave \add without\add* you.” So he got ready, and went with her.
\v 31 However, Gehazi had gone ahead and he put the staff on the young man’s face, but he didn’t open his eyes or make a sound. So he returned to meet Elisha and told him that the young man didn’t wake up.
\p
\v 32 When Elisha reached the house, it was a shock to see the young man lying on his bed, dead.
\v 33 He went in and he shut the door behind the other two, and he prayed to Yahweh.
\v 34 Then he got on the bed, and he lay on the child and bent down near him, putting his mouth on his mouth, and his eyes on his eyes, and his palms on his palms. Then the child’s body started getting warmer,
\v 35 and he got down and walked around the room several times. Then he got on the bed \add again\add*, and bent down near him. The young man gasped several times, and then opened his eyes.
\v 36 Then he called to Gehazi, and told him to call the Shunamit woman, and he called her, and she came and Elisha told her, “Here, take your son.”
\v 37 Then she came in and fell to her knees in front of him with her face to the ground. Then she picked up her son and went out,
\v 38 and Elisha returned to Gilgal.
\s1 Two more miracles
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p At that time, there was a drought and the prophets-in-training were sitting facing him, and Elisha told his servant, “Get the large pot and boil a stew for these prophets-in-training.”
\v 39 One \add person\add* went out to the countryside to gather herbs, and he found a wild vine, and he collected wild fruit from it—as many as he could hold in the fold of his robe. Then not realising that they were poisonous, he came and he sliced them into the pot of stew.
\v 40 Then they served it up for the men to eat, but when they ate it, they called out, “Man of God, there’s poison in that pot!”
\v 41 “Bring me some flour,” Elisha said. Then he threw it into the pot, and said, “Serve it up for the people to eat,” and no one was harmed.
\p
\v 42 \add One day\add* a man came from Baal-Shalishah, and he brought to the man of God an offering from the beginning of his harvest: twenty loaves of barley bread, and newly-harvested corn in his sack, and Elisha said \add to his servant\add*, “Give it to our people so they can eat.”
\p
\v 43 “How can I divide that out to one hundred men?” asked his servant.
\p “Give them to the people so they can eat,” he replied, “because Yahweh says that they can eat and have left-overs.”
\v 44 So he laid it out in front of them and they ate and had left-overs, just like Yahweh had said would happen.
\c 5
\s1 Naaman’s healing
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Now Naaman \add was\add* the captain in Syria’s army. \add He\add* was highly respected by Aram’s king, and was promoted to a high position because Yahweh had helped Aram win many battles because of him. However, despite his military prowess, he suffered from a serious skin disease called leprosy.
\v 2 \add Previously\add* troops that had been sent out from Aram, had taken a young Israelite girl captive and she had become Naaman’s wife’s servant.
\v 3 \add One day\add* she said to her mistress, “I wish that my master would go and see the prophet who \add lives\add* in Samaria, and then he would cure him of his leprosy.”
\v 4 \add In due course, Naaman heard\add* and went and told his master what the young Israelite girl had said.
\v 5 The king of Aram responded, “Ok, I’ll write a letter to the king of Israel for you to take.”
\p So Naaman set off, taking along seventy kilograms of gold, 300 kilograms of silver, and ten sets of \add fine\add* clothes,
\v 6 and he took the letter to the king of Israel. \add The letter\add* said, “Greetings. When this letter gets to you, see, I’ve sent my servant Naaman to you so that you can heal his skin disease.”
\p
\v 7 However when Israel’s king read the letter, he tore his clothes \add in despair\add*, and he said, “Do they think that I’m God who ends people’s lives and gives life to others? Why’s this person sending a man \add here for me\add* to take away his skin disease? Surely he must be creating a situation so he has an excuse to attack me!”
\p
\v 8 Later when Elisha the man of God heard that Israel’s king had torn his clothes, he sent a message to the king, “Why did you tear your clothes? Please, let that man come to me so he can know that there \em is\em* a prophet in Israel.”
\v 9 So Naaman came on his chariots and horses, and he stood at the entrance to Elisha’s house.
\v 10 Elisha sent a messenger \add out\add* to him to tell him, “Go and wash seven times in the Jordan \add river\add*, and then you’ll be better and your skin will go back to normal.”
\p
\v 11 But Naaman was furious, and he left, saying, “Listen, I said to myself that he’d surely come out and stand \add there\add* and call on the name of his god Yahweh, then he’d wave his hand over my body and take away the skin disease.
\v 12 Aren’t the Damascus rivers Avanah and Parpar better than any river in Israel? Can’t I bathe in them \add instead\add* and get healed?” So he turned and he went off angrily.
\v 13 Then \add some of\add* his servants approached him and suggested, “My master, if the prophet had told you to do something more significant, wouldn’t you have done it? So maybe you could do the simple thing that he told you: wash and get better.”
\p
\v 14 So Naaman went down to the Jordan \add river\add* and dipped in it seven times like the man of God had told him to. Then his skin became clear again, like the skin of a young boy, and he was healed.
\v 15 Then he returned to the man of God along with his entire retinue. Standing in front of him, he said, “Look, please. I know that there is no god in all the earth except in Israel. Now, please, accept a blessing from your servant.”
\p
\v 16 “By the life of Yahweh who I serve,” he responded, “I won’t take \add anything\add*.”
\p Naaman urged him to take something, but he kept refusing.
\v 17 Then Naaman said, “Well if not, please, \add at least\add* may we load a pair of mules with some soil \add to take back with us\add*, because \add from now on,\add* your servant will no longer make a burnt offering or a sacrifice to other gods—only to Yahweh.
\v 18 However, \add I’ll ask in advance:\add* May Yahweh forgive your servant when my master goes to the house of \add the god\add* Rimon to prostrate himself there, and he’s leaning on my hand, and I prostrate myself in the house of Rimon. When I prostrate myself in the house of Rimon, please may Yahweh forgive your servant for this one activity.”
\p
\v 19 “Go in peace,” Elisha replied.
\p After they’d gone a short distance,
\v 20 Elisha’s servant said \add to himself\add*, “Hmmh, my master wouldn’t take anything from that Naaman the Aramean. By the life of Yahweh, I think I should run after him and take something that he brought.”
\v 21 So Gehazi hurried after Naaman. When Naaman saw a person running after him, he got down from his chariot to meet him, and he said, “Is there a problem?”
\p
\v 22 “Peace,” he said, “My master sent me to tell you that two young prophets-in-training arrived from the Ephraim hill country. \add Can we\add* please have thirty kilograms of silver and two sets of clothing for them?”
\p
\v 23 “Sure,” said Naaman. “Take sixty kilograms,” he urged him. Then he separated sixty kilograms of silver into two bags along with two sets of clothes, then he gave them to two of his young men to carry back for him.
\v 24 When Gehazi reached the hill, he sent the men back and they returned \add to Naaman\add*. Then he took the sacks that they’d brought and hid them in the house.
\v 25 When he went back in to his master, Elisha asked him, “Where did you go, Gehazi?”
\p “Your servant hasn’t been anywhere,” he replied.
\p
\v 26 “Wasn’t I there in spirit when a man turned from on his chariot to meet you?” Elisha said to him, “Is this a time to take silver or clothing, or to take olive trees or vineyards, or sheep or cattle, or male or female servants?
\v 27 \add Because of that,\add* Naaman’s leprosy will transfer to you and to your descendants forever.” When Gehazi left the room, his skin had gone white as snow and he was now a leper.
\c 6
\s1 The floating axe head
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add One day,\add* the prophets-in-training said to Elisha, “Please look, The place where we’re sitting to learn from you is too narrow to fit us all in.
\v 2 Please, let us go down by the Jordan \add river\add*, and let each of us cut down and bring back a log from there to make a new classroom for us to sit in.”
\p “Go \add ahead\add*,” he answered.
\p
\v 3 One of them insisted, “Please, say that you’ll come with your servants.”
\p “\add Okay then,\add* I’ll come,” he said.
\v 4 So he went with them to the Jordan \add valley\add* to cut down \add some\add* trees,
\v 5 but while one of them was chopping down a tree, \add suddenly\add* the axe head \add separated from the handle and\add* went flying into the water. The man called out to Elisha, “Oh, Master, \add what should I do\add*? It’s not my axe—I borrowed it!”
\p
\v 6 “Where did it fall in?” the man of God asked. After the man had shown him the place, Elisha cut off \add a stick\add* and threw it there, and the iron \add axe head\add* rose to the surface.
\v 7 “Now get it out,” he said, and the man stretched out and grabbed it.
\s1 The Syrian army is defeated
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 8 \add In those days,\add* the king of Syria was fighting against Israel, and he’d instruct his servants, “Set up our camp in such and such a place.”
\v 9 However, the man of God sent to Israel’s king to warn him, “Beware of passing by such and such a place, because the Aramean \add army\add* will be attacking there.”
\v 10 So the king would send messengers to the people at that place to warn them to watch and be prepared.
\p That happened several times
\v 11 and the Aramean king got very upset about it. He called in his servants and demanded, “Which one of you \add is a traitor—passing military intelligence\add* to the king of Israel?”
\p
\v 12 “No, my master the king,” a servant answered. “It’s the prophet Elisha in Israel who tells their king the things that you say \add even\add* in your bedroom.”
\p
\v 13 “Go and find out where he is,” he \add instructed\add*, “and I’ll send some \add men\add* there to capture him.”
\p “He’s in Dotan,” he was told \add later\add*.
\v 14 So he sent chariots and horses and a large contingent, and they arrived there at night and surrounded the town.
\p
\v 15 Early \add the next morning\add*, the servant of the man of God got up, and going outside he was shocked to see chariots and horses and an army surrounding the town, so he called Elisha, “Oh, my master! What will we do?”
\p
\v 16 “Don’t be afraid,” he said, “because we have many more than that on our side.”
\v 17 Then Elisha prayed, “Yahweh, please enable his sight so he can see.” Then Yahweh enabled the eyes of the young man, and he was stunned to see that the hill was covered with chariots of fire and horses all around Elisha.
\p
\v 18 When the Arameans approached to swoop in, Elisha prayed to Yahweh, “Please, strike those warriors with blinding light.” So Yahweh struck them with blinding light just as Elisha had requested,
\v 19 and Elisha told them, “This isn’t the \add right\add* way, and this isn’t the town. Follow me, and let me lead you to the man you’re looking for.”
\p Then he led them to Samaria,
\v 20 and when they’d entered the city, Elisha said, “Yahweh, open the eyes of these people so they can see.” So Yahweh opened their eyes, and then they were stunned to see that they were in the middle of Samaria.
\p
\v 21 When he saw them, the king of Israel asked Elisha, “Should I kill them? Should I kill them, my father?”
\v 22 “\add No,\add* don’t kill them,” he replied. “Would you cold-bloodedly kill your prisoners of war? \add No,\add* give them bread and water and let them eat and drink, and then they can return to their master.”
\v 23 So the king prepared a large meal for them, and they ate and drank, and he sent them off to return to their master. After that, the Aramean troops didn’t continue their ventures into Israel.
\s1 Ben-Hadad besieges Samaria
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 24 Sometime after that, King Ben-Hadad of Syria assembled all of his warriors and attacked Samaria, and besieged it.
\v 25 Being surrounded like that \add caused\add* a terrible famine inside the city, until a donkey’s head would be sold for eighty silver \add coins\add*, and a cup of doves’ droppings was sold for five silver \add coins\add*.
\p
\v 26 One day as the king of Israel was walking past on the \add top of the city\add* wall, a woman cried out to him, “Save \add me\add*, my master, the king!”
\p
\v 27 “\add If\add* Yahweh won’t save you, how could I possibly save you?” he snapped. “\add Some grain\add* from the threshing floor or \add some wine\add* from the wine-pressing \add pit\add*?”
\v 28 Then he asked her, “\add Okay,\add* what’s your problem?”
\p “This woman said to me, ‘Let’s have your son and we’ll eat him today, then we’ll eat my son tomorrow,’ ” she answered.
\v 29 “So we boiled my son and ate him, and then the next day I said, ‘Hand over your son and we’ll eat him,’ but she’d hidden him.”
\p
\v 30 When he heard that, the king (standing up on the wall) tore his clothes, and the people could see to their astonishment that he was wearing coarse, abrasive sackcloth underneath,
\v 31 and he said, “May God do that to me and more, if Shafat’s son Elisha still has his head on him by the end of the day.”
\p
\v 32 Meanwhile, Elisha was sitting at home, joined by the \add city\add* elders. The king had sent a warrior ahead of him, and before he’d arrived at the house, Elisha had told the elders, “\add Well now,\add* that son of a murderer has sent an assassin to separate me from my head. Listen, when that hit-man arrives, shut the door and lean against it. His master won’t be far behind him.”
\v 33 He was still talking with them, when, wow, the warrior was coming towards the house \add soon followed by the king who\add* said, “Listen, Yahweh caused this disaster so why should I trust him any longer?”
\c 7
\p
\v 1 “Listen to what Yahweh says,” Elisha responded, “Yahweh says that at this time tomorrow, a drum of flour will be sold for a shekel, and two drums of barley will be sold for a shekel at the city gate.”
\p
\v 2 “That’s impossible,” interjected the captain at the king’s side. “Yahweh would have to make windows in the sky.”
\p “\add Oh, it’ll happen\add*,” the man of God answered, “\add and you’ll see it with your own eyes, but you won’t get to eat any of it\add*.”
\p
\v 3 \add That day,\add* four men \add who were isolated due to\add* a skin disease were at the city gate, and they asked each other, “Why are we \add just\add* sitting here until we starve?
\v 4 If we go into the city, we’ll starve to death in there, but if we sit here, we’ll die \add anyway\add*. Why don’t we go into the Aramean army camp—if they kill us then we’ll die, but if they let us live, then we’ll live.”
\v 5 So as it was getting dark, they went to go into the camp, but as they came to the edge of it, to their surprise there wasn’t a person in sight.
\v 6 \add It turned out that\add* the master had caused the Aramean army to hear the sounds of chariots and horses and a large army, so they’d said to each other that the king of Israel must have hired the Hittite and Egyptian kings to join together to fight them.
\v 7 As a result, they’d taken off and fled in the twilight. They’d abandoned their tents and horses and donkeys—leaving the camp just as it was, and fled for their lives.
\p
\v 8 So those \add four\add* men with leprosy approached the edge of the camp and went into the first tent, and ate and drank. Then they took some gold and silver and clothes out, and they went and hid it. Then they returned and went into another tent, and stole some stuff from that one, and went and hid that.
\v 9 Then they said to each other, “We shouldn’t really be doing this today. It’s a day of good news, but we’ve been keeping it to ourselves. \add If\add* we wait until the light of the morning, we’ll get punished for it, so let’s go now and inform the king’s household.”
\v 10 So they went and called out to one of the city gatekeepers, and they informed them, “We went to the Aramean camp, but listen, there wasn’t anyone there, or any voices. The horses and donkeys were still tethered, and the tents were left just as they were.”
\p
\v 11 The gatekeepers called out, and the news was reported to the palace.
\v 12 Although it was already night-time, the king got up and warned his servants, “I’ll tell you what’s going on here: The Arameans know that we’re hungry, so they left their camp to go and hide in the countryside, thinking that we’d go out from the city, and then they could capture us and take over the city.”
\p
\v 13 But one of his servants answered, “\add Sir,\add* please, let us take five of the remaining horses. They’re pretty much finished anyway, like the remaining population of Israel, \add so there’s not much to lose\add*. Let us go and investigate.”
\v 14 So with the king’s permission, they took two chariot horses and went to check out the Aramean camp.
\v 15 They went as far as the Jordan \add river\add*, and were amazed that the whole road was full of clothing and equipment that the Arameans had thrown off in their rush, and the messengers returned and informed the king.
\v 16 So the people went out and plundered the camp. Then it happened: a drum of fine flour was sold for a shekel, and two drums of barley for the same amount, just as Yahweh had said would happen.
\p
\v 17 The king had appointed the captain who had argued with Elisha to be in charge at the \add city\add* gate, but the people trampled him there and he died, just as the man of God had said when the king had gone to his place.
\v 18 When the man of God had told the king that two drums of barley would be sold for a shekel, and a drum of flour also sold for a shekel the next morning at the city gate,
\v 19 the captain \add was the one who’d\add* answered the man of God and had said that Yahweh would have to make windows in the sky to do that, and Elisha had told him that he’d see it, but wouldn’t be able to eat any of it.
\v 20 That’s exactly what happened, because he got trampled by the \add rush of\add* people at the gate and died.
\c 8
\s1 The Shunamit woman gets her land back
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 One day Elisha spoke to the \add Shunamit\add* woman whose son he’d brought back to life, “Pack up and leave here, you and your household, and go and stay somewhere else, because Yahweh’s going to send a seven-year drought.”
\v 2 So the woman got up, and she did what the man of God told her: She packed up her household and left to go and live in the Philistines’ region for seven years.
\p
\v 3 Then eventually after seven years, the woman returned from the Philistines’ region and she went to petition the king about her house and land.
\v 4 \add When she arrived,\add* the king had asked Gehazi, the servant of the man of God, “Please tell me all the incredible things that Elisha has done.”
\v 5 Then Gehazi was telling the king that Elisha had made the dead live, and how the woman who he made her son live was petitioning the king on behalf of her house and her field. Then Gehazi exclaimed, “My master, the king, this is the woman, and that’s her son, who Elisha made live!”
\v 6 So the king questioned the woman and she told him \add her story\add*. Then the king appointed a court official to attend to her case, saying, “Return everything that belongs to her plus the profit from her field from the day she left until now.”
\s1 Hazael murders Ben-Hadad
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 7 Elisha went to \add Syria’s\add* \add capital\add* Damascus at a time when King Ben-Hadad of Aram was \add very\add* sick, and someone told him that the man of God was in town.
\v 8 The king instructed \add an official named\add* Hazael, “Take a gift with you and go and meet the man of God. Then inquire from Yahweh through him to find out if I’ll recover from this sickness.”
\v 9 So Hazael went to meet Elisha and took presents with him loaded on forty camels—\add some of\add* every good thing \add that he could find\add* in Damascus. He went and stood in front of him and asked, “Your servant, King Ben-Hadad sent me to ask you if he’ll recover from his sickness?”
\p
\v 10 “Go \add and\add* tell him that he’ll surely live,” Elisha answered. “But Yahweh has shown me that he’ll definitely die.”
\v 11 Then Elisha stared at him until Hazael felt embarrassed, and then the man of God started to cry.
\p
\v 12 “Why are you crying, my master?” Hazael asked.
\p “Because I know what evil things you’ll do to the Israelites,” he said. “You’ll burn down their fortresses and kill their young men with the sword. You’ll smash their children’s \add heads on rocks\add*, and rip open \add the bellies of\add* their pregnant women.”
\p
\v 13 Hazael asked, “But how could your servant \add be in a position to\add* do such a major thing when he’s only minor, like a dog \add in the palace\add*?”
\p “Yahweh has let me see you as king over Syria.” Elisha replied.
\p
\v 14 Then he left Elisha and went back to his master who asked him, “What did Elisha tell you?”
\p And he answered, “He told me that you’ll certainly live.”
\v 15 the very next day, Hazael dipped Ben-Hadad’s blanket in water and held it over his face \add until\add* he died, and then Hazael started to reign in his place.
\s1 Jehoram reigns over Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 21:1-20)
\p
\v 16 In the fifth year of \add the reign of\add* Ahab’s son Joram as king of Israel, Jehoshaphat’s son Jehoram became king of Judah.
\v 17 He was thirty-two when he became king and he reigned from Jerusalem for eight years.
\v 18 He followed in the \add evil\add* ways of the kings of \add the northern\add* Israel \add kingdom\add*, just as Ahab’s descendants had done, because he’d married \add one of\add* Ahab’s daughters. He did what Yahweh had said was evil,
\v 19 but Yahweh wasn’t willing to destroy Judah, for the sake of his servant David—he’d promised David that his descendants would always rule Judah.
\p
\v 20 \add It was\add* during King Jehoram’s time that Edom rebelled from Judah’s control, and they appointed their own king.
\v 21 So Joram \add took his army and\add* chariots and crossed \add the valley\add* towards Tsa’ir \add in Edom\add*. They attacked at night, but as the Edomite army and chariots started to surround them, they \add had to\add* retreat back to their tents.
\v 22 So Edom has been out from under the control of Judah to this day. Then Livnah revolted at the same time.
\p
\v 23 Everything else that Joram said and did is written in the book of the events of the kings of Judah.
\v 24 Then Joram died and was buried with his ancestors in David’s city, and his son Ahazyah replaced him as king.
\s1 Ahazyah reigns over Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 22:1-6)
\p
\v 25 In the twelfth year of Ahab’s son Joram’s \add reign\add* over Israel, Jehoram’s son Ahazyah became the king of Judah.
\v 26 Ahazyah was twenty-two when he became king, and he reigned from Jerusalem for one year. (His mother’s name was Athaliah, the daughter of King Omri of Israel.)
\v 27 He followed Ahab’s customs and did what Yahweh had said was evil like Ahab’s descendants, because he was a son-in-law of a descendant of Ahab.
\v 28 He joined Ahab’s son Joram to battle against Syria’s King Hazael at Ramot-Gilead, but the Arameans wounded Joram.
\v 29 King Joram returned to Jezreel to recover from the wounds that Arameans had given him at Ramah when he’d fought with Aram’s King Hazael. \add Later,\add* King Ahazyah of Judah (Jehoram’s son), went down to see (Ahab’s son) Joram in Jezreel because he was wounded.
\c 9
\s1 Yihu is selected to rule Israel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 Then the prophet Elisha called to one of the prophets-in-training and told him, “Tuck your robe in, take this flask of oil, and hurry to Ramot-Gilead.
\v 2 When you get there, you’ll see Jehu the son of Nimshi’s son Jehoshaphat there. Take him into a private inner room, away from his brothers,
\v 3 and pour \add some of\add* the \add olive\add* oil over his head and tell him that Yahweh says that he’s anointed him as king over Israel. Then open the door and get out of there fast.”
\v 4 So the young prophet went to Ramot-Gilead,
\v 5 and when he arrived, he was surprised to see the army captains sitting there. He said, “I need to talk to you, captain.”
\p “Which one of us?” Jehu asked.
\p “It’s a message for \em you\em*, captain,” he said.
\v 6 So Jehu got up, and went over to the house where the prophet poured the oil on his head and told him, “Israel’s god Yahweh says that he’s anointed you as king over Yahweh’s people—over Israel.
\v 7 You should attack the house of Ahab your master so Yahweh can avenge the murder of his servants, the prophets, and avenge the murder of all his servants who were killed by Jezebel.
\v 8 All of Ahab’s household should be killed, and Yahweh won’t allow any of his male descendants to live, either before or after they’ve left home.
\v 9 Yahweh wants Ahab’s descendants to be eliminated like the descendants of Nevat’s son Jeroboam and Ahiyah’s son Baesha were.
\v 10 The dogs will eat Jezebel’s \add body\add* where she dies in Jezreel—she won’t be buried.” Then he opened the door and rushed away.
\p
\v 11 When Jehu came back \add to the room where\add* the servants of his masters were, and they asked him, “Is everything okay? What did that madman want with you?”
\p “You yourselves know that man and the sort of stuff that he says,” he said.
\p
\v 12 “You’re lying,” they insisted. “Tell us \add what he told you\add*.”
\p “\add Well,\add* he said several things,” Jehu answered, “and then he told me that Yahweh says he’s anointed me as king over Israel.”
\p
\v 13 Then they all spread their cloaks on the steps of the building for Jehu to walk out on, and they blew a trumpet and shouted, “Jehu is \add now\add* the king!”
\s1 The killing of Joram and Ahazyah
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 14 So Jehu the son of Nimshi’s son, Jehoshaphat conspired against Joram. Joram was \add busy\add* with the army guarding Ramot-Gilead from King Hazael of Syria.
\v 15 Then King Joram returned to Jezreel to heal from the wounds from the Arameans when he’d fought with their King Hazael.
\p So Jehu told his men, “If you all want me as king, then don’t let anyone leave this city \add of Ramot-Gilead\add* in case they go to Jezreel city and tell them our plans.”
\v 16 Then Jehu rode to Jezreel \add himself\add* because Joram was recuperating there in bed, and King Ahazyah had gone up \add north\add* from Judah to visit him.
\p
\v 17 The watchman was standing on the watchtower in Jezreel, and he could see Jehu’s party \add in the distance\add* as they began approaching \add the city\add*, and he reported, “I can see a large group.”
\p \add King\add* Joram ordered, “Send out a chariot and go and ask them if they’re coming in peace.”
\v 18 So the horse and chariot went out to meet them and called out, “The king wants to know if you’re all coming in peace?”
\p But Jehu answered, “What’s that got to do with you? Turn around and follow us.”
\p Meanwhile the watchman announced, “The messenger has met them, but he isn’t returning.”
\p
\v 19 So the king sent out a second horse and chariot, and he approached and asked, “The king wants to know if you’re all coming in peace?”
\p Again Jehu replied, “What’s that got to do with you? Turn around and follow us.”
\p
\v 20 Then the watchman declared, “He \add also\add* met with them but didn’t return. The leader is riding like Nimshi’s son Jehu, because he’s riding like crazy.”
\p
\v 21 “\add Quick,\add* get my chariot ready!” Joram ordered. They harnessed his chariot and King Joram of Israel and Judah’s King Ahazyah \add both\add* went out—each man in his chariot. They went out to meet Jehu and they found him at Navot’s field (the Yizre’eli).
\v 22 When Joram saw Jehu, he asked, “\add Have you come in\add* peace, Jehu?”
\p “How could there be peace,” he answered, “when your mother Jezebel is serving idols and embracing witchcraft?”
\p
\v 23 Then Joram swung the chariot reigns around and fled, shouting to Ahazyah, “It’s treason, Ahazyah!”
\v 24 Jehu grabbed his bow, and the arrow struck Joram between his shoulder blades and came out from his heart, and he slumped down \add dead\add* in his chariot.
\v 25 Then Jehu said to his third officer Bidkar, “Pick up his body and throw it out into the field of Navot, the Yizre’eli. I’m sure you remember how you and I were riding as pairs after his father Ahab, when Yahweh said this against him:
\v 26 ‘Yesterday I saw Navot and his sons killed here, declared Yahweh, and I will repay you in this very place, declares Yahweh.’ So now, throw his body down here because Yahweh declared that it would happen.”
\p
\v 27 Judah’s King Ahazyah saw \add that happen\add*, and he sped away through the garden by a house with Jehu chasing after him shouting, “Get him too in his chariot when he starts climbing upwards to Gur near Yivleam.” So as he fled towards Megiddo, he was killed there,
\v 28 and his servants took his body to Jerusalem, where they buried him in his ancestral tomb in David’s city.
\p
\v 29 (Ahazyah had become king over Judah in the eleventh year of the reign of Ahab’s son Joram \add over Israel\add*.)
\s1 The killing of Queen Jezebel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 30 Then Jehu came to Jezreel and \add Queen\add* Jezebel heard \add about it\add*. She painted her eyelids and did her hair nicely, then she looked \add down\add* through the window,
\v 31 and \add when\add* Jehu came in the gate, she said, “Greetings, Zimri \add the second\add*, murderer of his master.”
\v 32 Jehu looked up at the window and called out, “Who is with me? Who?” And two or three servants \add poked their heads out windows and\add* looked down to him.
\v 33 “\add Okay,\add*” he called up, “toss her out!”
\p So they tossed her \add out the window\add*, and some of her blood splashed onto the wall and onto the horses as she was trampled by them.
\v 34 Then Jehu \add and his men\add* went in and ate and drank, and then he said, “Now, you all better attend to that evil woman and bury her, because she’s a king’s daughter.”
\v 35 But when they went to bury her, they could only find her skull and her hands and feet.
\v 36 They came back inside and told him, “It’s just like what Yahweh said via his servant the Tishbi Elijah, when he said that the dogs would eat Jezebel’s flesh just outside Jezreel,
\v 37 and then her body would become like dung on the ground and no one would be able to recognise her specific bones.”
\c 10
\s1 Ahab’s descendants are eliminated
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 \add King\add* Ahab had seventy sons who lived in Samaria, so Jehu sent letters to the leaders and elders of Jezreel, and to the guardians of Ahab’s children, saying,
\v 2 “When you all receive this letter, you’ll have the sons of your masters with you, as well as chariots and horses, and weapons \add within\add* your fortified city.
\v 3 So select the best and the most honest one out of your masters’ sons \add or nephews\add*, and establish him on his father’s throne, and then be prepared to defend your masters’ family.”
\v 4 But they were extremely frightened and said, “Listen—those two kings couldn’t stand against Jehu, so how could we?”
\v 5 Then the man who was \add now\add* in charge of the household and leading the city elders sent \add a message\add* to Jehu, “We are your servants and we’ll do whatever you tell us to. We don’t plan to select a king. Do whatever you think best.”
\p
\v 6 So Jehu sent a second letter saying, “If you’re all on my side and ready to follow me, come and meet me at Jezreel tomorrow with the heads of your masters’ sons.”
\p The late King Ahab had seventy sons who were being brought up by the city leaders,
\v 7 and when that letter arrived, they killed and beheaded all of them, put the heads in baskets, and sent them to him at Jezreel.
\p
\v 8 A messenger went and informed Jehu that the heads had been brought, and he instructed them to pile them up in two piles at the \add city\add* gate until the next morning.
\v 9 In the morning he went out and stood there, and he told all the people, “You are innocent. Listen—it was me myself who conspired against my master and killed him. But who killed all these?
\v 10 \add You all should\add* know that when Yahweh says something will happen, then it will happen. He said via his servant Elijah that this is what would happen to Ahab’s family, and so it has.”
\v 11 Then Jehu killed all Ahab’s remaining family members in Jezreel, and all his chosen leaders, and his associates and priests, leaving no survivors.
\s1 The relatives of Ahazyah are killed
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 12 Then Jehu left \add Jezreel\add* and headed towards Samaria. When he reached the shepherds’ shearing house,
\v 13 he met the \add late\add* King Ahazyah’s brothers \add there\add*, and he asked them, “Who are you?”
\p “We’re \add King\add* Ahazyah’s brothers,” they said, “and we’re coming to look after the sons of the king and of the queen mother.”
\p
\v 14 “Arrest them,” he ordered, so they grabbed and slaughtered them near the pit at the shearing house. There were forty-two men and none were spared.
\s1 All Ahab’s other relatives are killed
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 15 Then continuing on from there, Jehu found Rekav’s son Yonadav \add coming\add* to meet him. Jehu greeted him and asked, “Are you wanting to do what is right just like I am?” IS THAT WHAT HE WAS REALLY ASKING???
\p “\add Yes,\add* I am.” Yonadav replied.
\p “Great,” Jehu said. “Give me your hand,” and he gave him his hand, and Jehu pulled him up to join him in the chariot,
\v 16 telling him, “Come with me and see my zeal for Yahweh.” So they rode together in the chariot.
\v 17 When they got to Samaria, Jehu killed the remainder of Ahab’s family who were there, until they were finally exterminated, just as Yahweh had said via Elijah.
\p
\v 18 Then Jehu assembled all the people and told them, “Ahab served Baal a little, \add but\add* Jehu will serve him a lot.
\v 19 So now, call all the prophets of Baal to come here—all his servants and all his priests. Don’t let anyone stay away because I’m going to make a large sacrifice to Baal. Anyone who’s absent will be executed.” However Jehu was being cunning in order to destroy Baal’s followers.
\v 20 Jehu ordered, “Announce a special holiday to assemble \add and worship\add* Baal,” and so they did.
\v 21 \add On that day,\add* Jehu brought in all the people, and all the servants of the Baal came, leaving no one out. They entered the temple of the Baal and it was packed full.
\v 22 He told the wardrobe supervisor, “Bring out uniforms for all the servants of the Baal,” so he brought robes out for them.
\v 23 Then Jehu and Rekav’s son Yonadav entered the temple of the Baal and instructed the servants of the Baal, “Make sure that it’s only the servants of Baal in here—we don’t want any servants of Yahweh.”
\v 24 So they entered to make sacrifices and burnt offerings.
\p Meanwhile, Jehu had stationed eighty men outside, and had told them, “Don’t let any of the ones I send you escape, or it will be \+em your\+em* life in place of theirs.”
\p
\v 25 Then when he finished making the burnt offering \add in the outer area\add*, Jehu told the guards and officers, “\add Come in now and\add* strike them \add down\add*. Don’t let any of them get out.” So they attacked them with swords, and the guards and officers threw the bodies over the fence. Then they went inside the temple,
\v 26 and pulled the sacred posts outside and burnt them.
\v 27 They broke down the pillar of the Baal, and they broke down the temple. Since then it’s been used as an out-house.
\p
\v 28 \add That’s how\add* Jehu put an end to Baal worship in Israel.
\v 29 There was only one of the things with which Nevat’s son Jeroboam had caused Israel to sin that Jehu didn’t destroy: the golden calves at Bethel and Dan.
\v 30 Then Yahweh told Jehu, “Because you did good by following my principles and doing what I’ve said is right, including what you did to Ahab’s family, you’ll have four generations sitting on Israel’s throne.”
\v 31 But Jehu didn’t take care to follow the instructions of Israel’s god Yahweh whole-heartedly—he didn’t reject all of Jeroboam’s evil customs which had caused Israel to sin.
\s1 Jehu’s death
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 32 During that period, Yahweh began to reduce the size of Israel’s territory and \add Syria’s\add* \add King\add* Hazael attacked along their border
\v 33 from the Jordan river in the east \add as far south as\add* Aroer \add city\add* on the Arnon river—\add capturing\add* the Gilead and Bashan regions where the Gad, Reuben, and Manasseh \add tribes\add* lived.
\p
\v 34 Everything else that Jehu said and did is written in the book of the events of the kings of Israel.
\v 35 Then Jehu died and was buried in their ancestral tomb in Samaria, and his son Yehoahaz replaced him as king.
\v 36 Jehu had ruled from Samaria as the king of Israel for twenty-eight years.
\c 11
\s1 Queen Ataliya’s reign over Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 22:10–23:15)
\p
\v 1 When Ahazyah’s mother Athaliah saw that her son had been killed, she went and had all of his descendants executed so that none of them could claim the throne.
\v 2 However, King Joram’s daughter Yehosheva took Yoash, the son of her sister Ahazyah, and hid him in the bedroom along with his wet-nurse. Although he was on the list of the king’s sons to be executed, they hid him from Athaliah, and \add so\add* he wasn’t killed.
\v 3 Then he was able to stay hidden with her in the temple for the six years while Athaliah ruled Judah.
\p
\v 4 In the seventh year, Yehoyada \add the priest\add* sent for the officers over the royal bodyguards and the palace bodyguards to come to him inside Yahweh’s temple. He made them agree to take an oath of \add secrecy and loyalty\add* in the temple, then he let them see the king’s son.
\v 5 Then he commanded them, “This is what you all need to do: On the Rest Day, one third of you must go and guard the king’s palace.
\v 6 The \add second\add* third must guard the Sur gate, and the remainder must stay behind the others roaming back and forth,
\v 7 and the two off-duty groups must guard the temple and protect the \add young\add* king.
\v 8 You’ll surround the king—each man holding his weapons at the ready right around him—killing any person who approaches your ranks, and accompanying the king when he goes out or comes in.
\p
\v 9 So the officers over the royal bodyguards did everything that the priest Yehoyada had commanded. Both the on-duty and the off-duty men fulfilled their roles under Yehoyada’s direction that Rest Day.
\v 10 The priest had \add taken\add* the spears and the shields out of the temple that had belonged to King David and given them to the officers over the royal bodyguards,
\v 11 and they all stood in position, each man holding his weapons ready in his hand, right around the palace and the temple and the altar, and the king.
\v 12 Then the priest brought \add young\add* Yoash out and put the crown on his head. He handed him the official scroll, and they anointed him and made him king. Then they clapped their hands and shouted, “Long live the king!”
\p
\v 13 When Athaliah heard the sound of the people and the guards, she came to the people \add gathered\add* at Yahweh’s temple
\v 14 and was stunned to see the \add young, new\add* king standing near the pillar as was the custom, with the captains and the trumpeters near the king, and all the people of the land rejoicing and sounding trumpets. Then Athaliah tore her clothes and shouted out, “Treachery! Treason!”
\v 15 Then Yehoyada the priest commanded the officials over the bodyguards and the army officers, “Bring her out away from these public buildings. Don’t kill her in the temple, but kill anyone who tries to rescue her.
\v 16 So they grabbed her by each arm and took her out through the entrance for the horses to the palace, and she was executed there.
\s1 Yehoyada’s reforms
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 23:16-21)
\p
\v 17 Then Yehoyada led the making of an agreement between Yahweh and the king and the people, for the people to serve Yahweh, and \add also\add* between the king and between the people.
\v 18 Then people from all over the country went to the temple of Baal and broke down the altars. They smashed his images to pieces and killed Matan the priest of the Baal in front of the altars. Meanwhile, \add Yehoyada\add* the priest placed guards over Yahweh’s temple.
\v 19 Then he organised the officers over the royal guard and the palace guards to bring the king down from the temple of Yahweh, and along with all the people, they entered the palace via the gate usually used by the king’s bodyguards, and he sat on the throne.
\v 20 All the people celebrated and the city was quiet because Athaliah had been put to death with the sword at the palace.
\p
\v 21 Yoash was \add only\add* seven when he became king \add of Judah\add*.
\c 12
\s1 Yoash reigns over Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 23:16-23)
\p
\v 1 Yoash became king in Jerusalem in the seventh year of Jehu’s \add reign over Israel\add*. (His mother was Tsivyah from Beersheba.) He reigned for forty years
\v 2 and all his life, he did what Yahweh had said was correct because he was instructed by Yehoyada the priest.
\v 3 However, they didn’t remove the hilltop shrines where the people continued sacrificing and burning incense.
\p
\v 4 Then Yoash told the priests, “You all must collect all the money that’s donated to Yahweh’s temple, whether it’s the money they’re required to pay or the money that they themselves decide to give as sacred offerings to buy things for the temple.
\v 5 Each priest must bring what they’ve collected from their acquaintances, and use it to make repairs wherever they find that there’s been damage to the temple.”
\p
\v 6 However, \add when\add* the priests \add still\add* hadn’t repaired the damaged portions by the twenty-third year of Yoash’s reign,
\v 7 he sent for Yehoyada the priest and the \add other\add* priests, and questioned them, “Why aren’t you all strengthening the temple where it was damaged? From now on, you all won’t take any money from acquaintances, but you’ll give it directly for repairing the damage.”
\v 8 So the priests agreed to not accept money from the people, and that they themselves wouldn’t be the ones to repair the temple.
\p
\v 9 Then Yehoyada the priest took a \add wooden\add* chest and he made a hole in the top, and he placed it at the right of the altar. When a person came into the temple, the priests guarding the entrance would place any donated money into the chest.
\v 10 Whenever they noticed the chest getting full, the king’s secretary and the high priest would go and count the money as they bagged it up.
\v 11 They used the funds to pay the supervisors and inspectors and the tradesmen doing the repair work: carpenters and builders,
\v 12 and the masons and stone-cutters. They also paid for the timber and stones, and everything else that was needed for the temple repairs.
\v 13 However, they didn’t use the donated silver coins that were brought to the temple to make silver bowls, snuffers, basins, or trumpets, or for any other gold or silver instruments within the temple,
\v 14 because they passed on the donations to the people doing the work, and in this way Yahweh’s residence got repaired.
\v 15 They didn’t audit the men who took the silver coins to the workers because the whole system was based on trust.
\v 16 The silver \add coins that came as part\add* of the guilt offering and the sin offerings were not used for temple repairs—they belonged to the priests.
\p
\v 17 Then Syria’s King Hazael attacked Gath and captured it, after which he decided to attack Jerusalem.
\v 18 Judah’s King Yoash took all the sacred things that his ancestors Jehoshaphat and Jehoram and Ahazyah, the \add previous\add* kings of Judah, had consecrated and his sacred things, and all of the gold that was found in Yahweh’s temple treasuries and in the king’s palace, and he sent them to Aram’s King Hazael who then cancelled his planned attack on Jerusalem.
\p
\v 19 Everything else that Yoash said and did is written in the book of the events of the kings of Judah.
\p
\v 20 However, his servants got together and planned his assassination, and they ambushed Yoash in Beyt-Milo on the \add road\add* going down to Sila.
\v 21 His servants Yozavad (Shim’at’s son) and Yehozavad (Shomer’s son) struck him and he died, and they buried him in his ancestral tomb in David’s city, and his son Amatsyah replaced him as king.
\c 13
\s1 Yehoahaz reigns over Israel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 In the twenty-third year of Ahazyah’s son Yoash \add reigning\add* over Judah, Jehu’s son Yehoahaz became king over Israel and reigned seventeen years from Samaria.
\v 2 He did what Yahweh had said was evil and he imitated the customs of Nevat’s son Jeroboam who had caused Israel to sin—Yehoahaz didn’t end them.
\v 3 So Yahweh got angry with Israel and he allowed them to be defeated by Syria’s King Hazael and his son Ben-Hadad throughout Yehoahaz’s reign.
\v 4 Then Yehoahaz prayed to Yahweh for help, and Yahweh listened to him because he saw how much Israel was being oppressed by the Aramean king.
\v 5 So Yahweh sent a saviour to Israel, and they were able to stand up against the Arameans, and \add only then were\add* the Israelites able to live peacefully again from day to day.
\v 6 Despite that, they didn’t stop imitating the sins of Jeroboam’s family who caused Israel to sin—\add no,\add* Yehoahaz kept doing them, and even the Asherah pole \add remained\add* standing in Samaria.
\p
\v 7 The Aramean king had thoroughly destroyed \add much of Israel’s forces\add* \add only\add* leaving fifty horsemen, ten chariots, and ten thousand foot soldiers for Yehoahaz.
\p
\v 8 Everything else that Yehoahaz said and did, including his great achievements, is written in the book of the events of the kings of Israel.
\v 9 Then Yehoahaz died and was buried in Samaria, and his son Yehoash replaced him as king.
\s1 Yehoash reigns over Israel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 10 In the thirty-seventh year of King Yoash \add reigning\add* over Judah, Yehoahaz’s son Yehoash became king over Israel and \add reigned\add* sixteen years from Samaria.
\v 11 He did what Yahweh had said was evil and he imitated the customs of Nevat’s son Jeroboam who had caused Israel to sin—Yehoash didn’t end them.
\v 12 Everything else that Yehoash said and did, including his great achievements fighting along with Judah’s King Amatsyah, is written in the book of the events of the kings of Israel.
\v 13 Then Yehoash died and was buried in Samaria with the \add former\add* kings of Israel, and \add his son\add* Jeroboam replaced him on the throne.
\s1 Elisha’s death
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 14 Meanwhile Elisha had been sick and heading for his last days, so Israel’s king Yehoash had been to see him, and cried as he stood in front of him and said, “My father, my father, Israel’s chariot and its horsemen!”\f + \fr 13:14 \ft This was the same as what Elisha had said when Elijah had been taken to heaven. See 2:12.\f*
\p
\v 15 Then Elisha had told him, “Bring a bow and arrows.” So he’d brought them,
\v 16 and he’d told Israel’s king, “Grab the bow ready to draw.” So he did that, and then Elisha put his hands over the king’s hands.
\v 17 Then he said, “Open that window facing east.” The king opened it and Elisha said, “Shoot.” So he shot the arrow and Elisha said, “\add That’s\add* an arrow of victory for Yahweh and an arrow of victory against Syria. You will thoroughly defeat Aram at Afek.”
\v 18 Then he continued, “Take \add the rest of\add* the arrows.” Israel’s king took them, and Elisha told him, “Strike the ground \add with them\add*.” So he banged \add the arrows on the ground\add* three times.
\v 19 But the man of God was angry with him, and said, “You should have bashed them five or six times, then you would have bashed Aram up completely, but now you’ll defeat them three times.”
\p
\v 20 Then Elisha died and was buried.
\p Every year in the spring, bands of Moabites would make raids on Israel,
\v 21 and once there was \add a group of Israelites\add* burying a man. When they saw the raiding party, they \add quickly\add* threw the body into Elisha’s tomb, and when it touched his bones, the man came back to life and stood up.
\s1 War between Israel and Syria
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 22 Syria’s King Hazael oppressed Israel all through Yehoahaz’s reign,
\v 23 but Yahweh showed favour to the Israelites and felt compassionate towards them. Because of his agreement with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob, he didn’t want to destroy them, and he hasn’t tossed them out even to this day.
\p
\v 24 When Aram’s King Hazael died, his son Ben-Hadad replaced him as king.
\v 25 Then Yehoahaz’s son Yehoash attacked Aram three times, and he took \add back\add* the cities from Hazael’s son Ben-Hadad that had been taken in the war in his father Yehoahaz’s time.
\c 14
\s1 Amatsyah reigns over Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 25:1-24)
\p
\v 1 In the second year of the reign of Yehoahaz’s son Yehoash over Israel, Amatsyah replaced his father Yoash as king of Judah.
\v 2 He was twenty-five when he became king, and he reigned from Jerusalem for twenty-nine years. (His mother’s name was Yeho’adin from Jerusalem.)
\v 3 He did what Yahweh had said was correct behaviour, although not as thoroughly as his ancestor David—his behaviour was more like that of his father Yoash.
\v 4 However, the hilltop shrines weren’t removed—people were still sacrificing and burning incense at them.
\p
\v 5 Once he was firmly established as king, Amatsyah had the servants executed who had assassinated his father Yoash,
\v 6 but he didn’t have their sons executed, because in the scroll where Moses had written the laws, Yahweh had commanded, “Fathers shouldn’t be executed for what their sons do, nor should sons be executed for the crimes of their ancestors—rather an individual should only be executed for their own crime.”
\p
\v 7 He led the victory over ten thousand Edomites in the Dead valley, seizing Sela in the battle and renaming it as Yakete’el which it’s still called today.
\p
\v 8 Then Amatsyah sent messengers to Israel’s King Yehoash (the son of Yehoahaz, the son of Jehu challenging, “Come on, let’s have it out with each other.”
\v 9 But King Yehoash replied to Judah’s King Amatsyah, “The thistle that was in the Lebanon \add forest\add* sent to the cedar that was in the Lebanon, saying, ‘Give your daughter to my son as a wife.’ But a wild animal that was in the Lebanon passed by and it trampled the thistle.
\v 10 It’s true that you won a battle with Edom and you’re feeling encouraged. Accept that honour, but stay home \add now\add*. Why would you stir up trouble only to fall again—you and \add all\add* Judah with you?”
\p
\v 11 But Amatsyah wouldn’t listen so \add the armies of\add* Israel’s King Yehoash and Judah’s King Amatsyah faced each other in Judah at Beyt-Shemesh.
\v 12 However, Judah was overcome by Israel, and its warriors had to flee home \add from the battlefield\add*.
\v 13 So Israel’s King Yehoash captured Judah’s King Amatsyah (the son of Yehoash, the son of Ahazyah) in Beyt-Shemesh. Then he went to Jerusalem, and he broke down the city wall from the Ephraim gate up to the Corner gate—almost two hundred metres of it.
\v 14 He took all the gold and silver, and all the equipment that was found in Yahweh’s temple and in the palace treasuries. Then taking some hostages as well, he returned to Samaria.
\p
\v 15 Everything else that Yehoash said and did, including his battle with Judah’s King Amatsyah, is written in the book of the events of the kings of Israel.
\v 16 Then Yehoash died and was buried in Samaria with the \add former\add* kings of Israel, and his son Jeroboam replaced him as king.
\s1 The death of Judah’s King Amatsyah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 25:25-28)
\p
\v 17 Judah’s King Amatsyah (Yoash’s son) lived \add another\add* fifteen years after the death of Israel’s King Yehoash (Yehoahaz’s son).
\v 18 Everything else that Amatsyah said is written in the book of the events of the kings of Judah.
\p
\v 19 \add Some people\add* in Jerusalem had plotted to assassinate him, but he fled to Lakish. However, they followed him there and killed him in the city.
\v 20 His body was carried back on horses, and he was buried in his ancestral tomb in Jerusalem in David’s city.
\v 21 Then all the people of Judah took sixteen year old Azaryah and made him king to replace his \add late\add* father Amatsyah.
\v 22 Azaryah built up Eylot and he reestablished it as part of Judah before he died.
\s1 Jeroboam II reigns over Israel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 23 In the fifteenth year of Yoash’s son King Amatsyah’s \add reign\add* over Judah, Yehoash’s son Jeroboam became king of Israel \add and reigned from\add* Samaria for forty-one years.
\v 24 He did what Yahweh had said was evil—he imitated the customs of Nevat’s son Jeroboam who’d caused Israel to sin.
\v 25 Jeroboam restored Israel’s border from Levo-Hamat \add through\add* to the Dead sea, as Israel’s god Yahweh had foretold via his servant Jonah—the son of the prophet Amitay from Gath-Hahefer.
\v 26 \add That was\add* because Yahweh had seen how Israel had suffered badly and had been unable to control their own destinies, and that no other country would help them.
\v 27 But Yahweh had said that he wouldn’t allow Israel to be destroyed, so he’d used Yehoash’s son Jeroboam to save them.
\p
\v 28 Everything else that Jeroboam said and did, including how he fought and restored Damascus and Hamat to Israel, is written in the book of the events of the kings of Israel.
\v 29 Then Jeroboam died \add and\add* was buried with the \add former\add* kings of Israel, and his son Zechariah replaced him as king.
\c 15
\s1 Azaryah/Uzziah reigns over Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 26:1-23)
\p
\v 1 In the twenty-seventh year of King Jeroboam’s \add reign\add* over Israel, Amatsyah’s son Azaryah\f + \fr 15:1 \ft Azaryah was also known as Uzziyah, especially from v13 onwards.\f* became king of Judah.
\v 2 He was sixteen when he became king, and he reigned from Jerusalem for fifty-two years. (His mother’s name was Yekaleyah from Jerusalem.)
\v 3 He did what Yahweh had said was correct behaviour like his father Amatsyah had done,
\v 4 although the hilltop shrines weren’t removed—the people continued to sacrifice at them and burn incense.
\v 5 Yahweh caused Azaryah to become a leper and he had to live separately from others for the rest of his life, so his son Jotham ran the palace \add and\add* dealt with the people’s problems.
\p
\v 6 Everything else that Azaryah said and did is written in the book of the events of the kings of Judah.
\v 7 Azaryah died and was buried in the ancestral tomb in David’s city, and his son Jotham replaced him as king.
\s1 Zechariah reigns over Israel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 8 In the thirty-eighth year of King Azaryah’s \add reign\add* over Judah, Jeroboam’s son Zechariah became king over Israel \add and ruled for\add* six months from Samaria.
\v 9 He did what Yahweh had said was evil, just as his ancestors had done—he didn’t avoid the kinds of behaviour that had been done by Nevat’s son Jeroboam who had caused Israel to sin.
\v 10 Then Yavesh’s son Shalum plotted to assassinate him, and he attacked him in front of the people and killed him, and he replaced him as king.
\p
\v 11 Everything else that Zechariah said and did is written in the book of the events of the kings of Israel.
\p
\v 12 \add Previously\add* Yahweh had told \add King\add* Jehu that his descendants would sit on the throne for four generations, and that had come true.
\s1 Shalum reigns over Israel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 13 Yavesh’s son Shalum became king in the thirty-ninth year of King Azaryah’s \add reign\add* over Judah, but he \add only\add* reigned from Samaria for one month.
\v 14 Then Gadi’s son Menahem came to Samaria from Tirtsah, and he attacked Yavesh’s son Shalum \add there\add* in Samaria and killed him, replacing him as king.
\v 15 Everything else that Shalum said, including his assassination plot, is written in the book of the events of the kings of Israel.
\v 16 At that time, Menahem attacked Tifsah and everyone in \add the city\add*, and its borders from Tirtsah, because they wouldn’t open their gates. Then he ripped open the bellies of all the pregnant women \add there\add*.
\s1 Menahem’s reign over Israel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 17 In the thirty-ninth year of King Azaryah’s \add reign\add* over Judah, Gadi’s son Menahem became king over Israel \add and he reigned\add* from Samaria for ten years.
\v 18 He did what Yahweh had said was evil—he didn’t avoid the kinds of behaviour that had been done by Nevat’s son Jeroboam who had caused Israel to sin.
\p During his reign,
\v 19 King Pul of Assyria attacked Israel, and Menahem gave Pul over thirty tonnes of silver, so he would support Menahem’s reign.
\v 20 (Menahem had taxed all the wealthy families across Israel—fifty silver coins per adult male.) As a result, the Assyrian king cancelled his plans to attack and returned home.
\p
\v 21 Everything else that Menahem said and did is written in the book of the events of the kings of Israel.
\v 22 Then Menahem died and was buried, and his son Pekahyah replaced him as king.
\s1 Pekahyah reigns over Israel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 23 In the fiftieth year of King Azaryah’s \add reign\add* over Judah, Menahem’s son Pekahyah became king over Israel \add and reigned\add* from Samaria for two years.
\v 24 He did what Yahweh had said was evil—he didn’t avoid the kinds of behaviour that had been done by Nevat’s son Jeroboam who had caused Israel to sin.
\v 25 But Pekah the son of Remalyah, Pekahyah’s third officer, plotted to assassinate him, and he attacked him in the palace in Samaria with the assistance of Argov and Aryeh and fifty Giladim. So Pekah killed King Pekahyah and replaced him as king.
\v 26 Everything else that Pekahyah said and did is written in the book of the events of the kings of Israel—look at it.
\s1 Pekah’s reign over Israel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 27 In the fifty-second year of King Azaryah’s \add reign\add* over Judah, Remalyah’s son Pekah became king over Israel \add and reigned\add* from Samaria for twenty years.
\v 28 He did what Yahweh had said was evil—he didn’t avoid the kinds of behaviour that had been done by Nevat’s son Jeroboam who had caused Israel to sin.
\p
\v 29 During the reign of King Pekah over Israel, Assyria’s King Tiglat-Pileser came, and he captured \add the cities of\add* Iyon, Abel-Beyt-Ma’akah, Yanoah, Kedesh, and Hatsor, and \add the regions of\add* Gilead, Galilee, and Naphtali, and he exiled their people to Assyria.
\p
\v 30 Then Elah’s son Hoshea plotted to assassinate Remalyah’s son Pekah, and he attacked him, killing him and replacing him as king in the twentieth year of \add King\add* Jotham’s \add reign over Judah\add*.
\v 31 Everything else that Pekah said and did is written in the book of the events of the kings of Israel.
\s1 Jotham’s reign over Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 27:1-9)
\p
\v 32 In the second year of Remalyah’s son Pekah’s \add reign\add* over Israel, Azaryah’s son Jotham began to reign over Judah.
\v 33 He was twenty-five when he became king, and he reigned from Jerusalem for sixteen years. (His mother’s name was Yerusha, the daughter of Zadok.)
\v 34 He did what Yahweh had said was correct behaviour like his father Azaryah/Uzziah had done,
\v 35 \add although\add* the hilltop shrines weren’t removed—the people continued to sacrifice at them and burn incense. Jotham built the upper gate to Yahweh’s temple.
\p
\v 36 Everything else that Jotham said and did is written in the book of the events of the kings of Judah.
\v 37 In those days, Yahweh began to send Syria’s King Retsin and Remalyah’s son Pekah against Judah.
\v 38 Then Jotham died and was buried in their ancestral tomb in the city of his ancestor David, and his son Ahaz replaced him as king.
\c 16
\s1 Ahaz’s reign over Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 28:1-27)
\p
\v 1 In the seventeenth year of Remalyah’s son Pekah’s \add reign over Israel\add*, Jotham’s son Ahaz began to reign over Judah.
\v 2 Ahaz was twenty when he became king and he reigned from Jerusalem for sixteen years, but he didn’t follow what his god Yahweh had said was correct behaviour like his ancestor David had done.
\v 3 \add Actually\add* he followed the behaviour of the kings of Israel, and he even sacrificed his son as a burnt offering like they do in the nations that Yahweh hated and which Yahweh had driven out of the land as the Israelites had entered.
\v 4 Also he sacrificed on the hilltop shrines and burnt incense on them, and on the hills and under every large tree.
\p
\v 5 Then Syria’s King Retsin and Israel’s King Pekah (Remalyah’s son) came uphill to attack Jerusalem, and they laid siege against \add King\add* Ahaz but they weren’t able to conquer \add the city\add*.
\v 6 At that time, Aram’s King Retsin recaptured Eylot \add city\add* for Aram, then he drove the Judeans out of Eylot and Arameans moved in \add instead\add* and they have lived there to this day.
\v 7 So Ahaz sent messengers to Assyria’s King Tiglat-Pileser, “I’m your servant and your son. Come up and rescue me from the kings of Aram and Israel who are here attacking me.”
\v 8 Ahaz took the gold and silver from Yahweh’s temple and from the palace treasuries, and sent it as a gift to the Assyrian king.
\v 9 The king of Assyria listened to him and went in and attacked Damascus, and he captured it and exiled its people to Kir, and he executed \add King\add* Retsin.
\p
\v 10 King Ahaz went to meet the Assyrian King Tiglat-Pileser in Damascus, and he saw the altar that was there. So he sent a drawing and the detailed measurements of the altar to the priest Uriah.
\v 11 So Uriah built the altar according to the plans that King Ahaz had sent from Damascus and had it finished before King Ahaz returned from Damascus.
\v 12 When the king got back to Jerusalem and saw the altar, he went up onto it
\v 13 and made his burnt offering and his grain offering, and he poured out his drink offering, and he sprinkled the blood of his peace offerings onto the altar.
\v 14 He got the bronze altar that had been dedicated to Yahweh moved back away from the temple and placed beside the \add newer, bigger\add* altar.
\v 15 Then King Ahaz ordered Uriah, “Use the large altar for the morning burnt offerings and the evening grain offerings, and for the king’s burnt offerings and grain offerings, and for the people’s burnt offerings and grain offerings and drink offerings. Use it for sprinkling all the blood of the burnt offerings and of the sacrifices on. But I will use the bronze altar for seeking \add guidance\add*.”
\v 16 So Uriah the priest put everything into effect that King Ahaz had commanded.
\p
\v 17 Then King Ahaz cut the frames off the stands \add outside the temple\add*, and he removed the basins off them. He took down ‘The Sea’ that had been sitting on top of bronze bulls and put it on the stone floor.
\v 18 He also removed the canopy that had been built at the temple for use on the Rest Days, and \add blocked up\add* the king’s outer entrance to the temple so that the Assyrian king couldn’t use it.
\p
\v 19 Everything else that Ahaz said and did is written in the book of the events of the kings of Judah.
\v 20 Then Ahaz died and was buried in their ancestral tomb in David’s city, and his son Hezekiah replaced him as king.
\c 17
\s1 Hoshea’s reign over Israel
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 1 In the twelfth year of King Ahaz’s \add reign\add* over Judah, Elah’s son Hoshea became king over Israel \add and he reigned\add* from Samaria for nine years.
\v 2 He did what Yahweh had said was evil, although nothing like the Israelite kings who’d preceded him.
\v 3 Assyria’s King Shalmaneser attacked \add and defeated\add* him, and Hoshea became his servant and paid tribute to him.
\v 4 But the Assyrian king discovered that Hoshea was planning a revolt and had sent messengers to the Egyptian King So and hadn’t kept up the annual tribute payment. So the Assyrian king arrested Hoshea and imprisoned him.
\s1 The fall of Samaria
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 5 Then the Assyrian king attacked the whole country, including besieging Samaria for three years.
\v 6 In the ninth year of Hoshea’s \add reign\add*, the Assyrian king captured Samaria. He exiled the Israelites to Assyria and settled them in Halah, and in Havor along the Gozan river, and in the cities of the Medes.
\p
\v 7 That happened because the Israelites had sinned against their god Yahweh who had rescued them from King Pharaoh in Egypt. They had sinned by worshipping other gods
\v 8 and imitating the customs of the people groups who Yahweh had driven out of the country ahead of them. They had also followed their kings \add in doing evil things\add*.
\v 9 The Israelites had done things secretly that their god Yahweh had said weren’t right, and they’d built hilltop shrines everywhere from the largest fortified cities to the smallest towns.
\v 10 They’d \add also\add* erected \add stone\add* pillars \add to honour gods\add* and Asherah poles on every high hill and under every large tree,
\v 11 and they’d burnt incense there on all those hilltops \add just\add* like the people groups that Yahweh had driven off the land ahead of them—doing those evil things had made Yahweh angry.
\v 12 They’d served idols even when Yahweh had forbidden them from doing that.
\p
\v 13 So Yahweh had warned Israel and Judah by the declarations by all of his prophets who said, “Turn from your evil ways and obey my commands and statutes, following all of the instructions that I gave your ancestors and which I delivered to you through my servants, the prophets.”
\v 14 However, they hadn’t listened and had hardened their attitudes \add just\add* like their ancestors who wouldn’t put their trust in their god Yahweh.
\v 15 They rejected his statutes and the agreement that he made with their ancestors, and the warning that he gave them. They chose to worship lifeless objects, and they became hollow and empty themselves, just like \add the people of\add* the surrounding countries which Yahweh had commanded them not to imitate.
\v 16 They disobeyed the instructions given to them by their god Yahweh by molding two metal calves for themselves and making an Asherah pole. Then they bowed down and worshipped the sun, moon, and stars, and they served Baal.
\v 17 They sacrificed their sons and daughters, and used fortune-tellers and witchcraft—making themselves slaves to evil by doing what Yahweh had forbidden, and so making him angry.
\v 18 So \add that’s why\add* Yahweh had become very angry at \add the northern\add* Israel \add kingdom\add* and caused them to be removed from the land. Only \add the Southern\add* Judah \add kingdom\add* remained,
\v 19 but even they didn’t obey the commands of their god Yahweh, but copied much of the bad behaviour of the northern kingdom.
\v 20 So Yahweh rejected all the Israelites and he caused them to suffer, including being plundered by enemy armies, until he banished them from his presence.
\p
\v 21 Firstly he’d torn the northern kingdom out from the leadership of David’s descendants and they’d made Nevat’s son Jeroboam king. Then Jeroboam had driven them away from following Yahweh, and had induced them into serious sin.
\v 22 So the Israelites had followed Jeroboam into sin and then didn’t turn away from it,
\v 23 and finally Yahweh had removed \add the people of the northern\add* Israel \add kingdom\add* from his presence just like he’d warned he would through the messages of his servants, the prophets. So the people were deported to Assyria \add where they remain\add* to this day.
\s1 The Assyrians settle in the northern kingdom
\s4 Northern kingdom
\p
\v 24 Then the Assyrian king sent people from Babylon and from Kut and from Ivah and from Hamat and Sefarvayim, and he settled them in the cities of the northern kingdom in place of the Israelites. And they possessed Samaria and lived in its towns.
\v 25 At first the new inhabitants living there didn’t respect Yahweh, however he sent lions among them and they started killing them.
\v 26 They told the Assyrian king, “The people groups that you exiled \add from their own countries\add* and settled in the towns around Samaria, don’t know the customs of the god of the land. So he’s sent lions against them, and look at them—killing them because they don’t know the customs of the god of the land.”
\v 27 “Send one of the priests back that was brought from there,” the Assyrian king commanded. “He can go back there and settle there \add again\add*, then he can teach them the customs of the god of that place.”
\v 28 So they found one of the priests who had been exiled from Samaria and sent him back \add to Israel\add*. He settled in Bethel and taught the people how Yahweh \add wanted to be\add* served.
\p
\v 29 But each people group had made their own gods and put them in the hilltop shrines that the Israelites had made \add before being exiled\add*. Each people group did that in the area where they’d been resettled.
\v 30 Those from Babylon made \add their god\add* Sukot-Benot, those from Kut made Nergal, those from Hamat made \add their god\add* Ashima,
\v 31 and the Avim made Nivhaz and Tartak. The Sefarvim sacrificed their \add own\add* sons in the fire to their gods Adramelek and Anamelek.
\v 32 \add At the same time,\add* they tried placating Yahweh, but they \add also\add* made their own priests to make sacrifices for them in their hilltop shrines.
\v 33 So they were worshipping Yahweh along with their \add own\add* gods—they’d brought those customs with them from the countries they’d been exiled from
\v 34 and which they \add still\add* follow to this day.
\p They’re not \add actually\add* obeying Yahweh because they’re not obeying the statutes or laws, or instructions or behaviour that Yahweh commanded the descendants of Jacob who he’d renamed to ‘Israel’).
\v 35 Yahweh had made an agreement with them and had commanded them, “You all must not honour other gods or bow to them. You all must not serve them or sacrifice to them.
\v 36 \add Only\add* serve Yahweh who used his incredible power to rescue you all out of Egypt. Honour him, and \add only\add* bow to him and sacrifice to him.
\v 37 Always take care to obey the statutes and customs, and the law and the instructions that he wrote for you, and you all must not honour other gods.
\v 38 Don’t forget the agreement that I made with you all, and don’t honour other gods—
\v 39 \add only\add* your god Yahweh, and then he himself will rescue you from all your enemies.”
\v 40 However, they didn’t listen, preferring to follow the customs from their former places of residence.
\p
\v 41 So those people groups honoured Yahweh, but also served their idols. That continued through the generations with their descendants continuing the same behaviour.
\c 18
\s1 Hezekiah’s reign over Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 29:1-2; 31:1)
\p
\v 1 In the third year of Elah’s son King Hoshea’s \add reign\add* over Israel, Ahaz’s son Hezekiah became king of Judah.
\v 2 He was twenty-five when he became king and he reigned from Jerusalem for twenty-nine years. (His mother was Zechariah’s daughter Avi.)
\v 3 He did what Yahweh had said was correct behaviour like his ancestor \add King\add* David had done.
\v 4 He demolished the hilltop shrines and shattered their pillars, and he cut down the Asherah poles. He crushed the bronze serpent that Moses had made, because the Israelites had named it ‘Nehushtan’ and had been offering incense to it until then.
\v 5 Hezekiah \add fully\add* trusted in Israel’s god Yahweh, and no other \add king\add* was like him among all the kings of Judah that either preceded or followed him.
\v 6 He relied completely on Yahweh—not turning away from following him, and he obeyed the instructions that Yahweh had commanded Moses.
\v 7 So Yahweh helped him and he was successful in everything he did. He rebelled against the Assyrian king and \add refused to submit to his demands\add*.
\v 8 He attacked and defeated the Philistines as far as Gaza and its borders—both the smaller towns and the fortified city.
\p
\v 9 Then in the fourth year of King Hezekiah’s reign (it was the seventh year of Elah’s son Hoshea’s \add reign\add* over Israel), the Assyrian King Shalmaneser had attacked Samaria and besieged it.
\v 10 They \add had finally\add* captured the city after three years. That was the sixth year of Hezekiah’s \add reign over Judah\add* and the ninth year of Hoshea’s \add reign\add* over Israel.
\v 11 So that was when the Assyrian king had exiled \add the people of\add* the northern Israel kingdom to Halah and to Havor along the Gozan river, and to the cities of the Medes.
\v 12 \add That happened\add* because they didn’t obey their god Yahweh, but \add instead\add* they broke the agreement with him—everything that Yahweh’s servant Moses had commanded. They didn’t take notice of it and they didn’t obey it.
\s1 Sennacherib invades Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 32:1-19; Isa 36:1-22)
\p
\v 13 In the fourteenth year of King Hezekiah’s \add reign\add*, Assyrian King Sennacherib attacked all the fortified cities in Judah and captured them.
\v 14 So King Hezekiah of Judah sent \add messengers\add* to the Assyrian king at Lakish, saying, “I apologise for my mistake. Stop attacking me \add and\add* I’ll give you whatever you demand of us.” Then the Assyrian king demanded a tribute of ten tonnes of gold and ten tonnes of silver.
\v 15 So Hezekiah gave him all the silver out of the temple and from the palace treasuries.
\v 16 He cut the doors off Yahweh’s temple and the pillars that he’d overlaid \add gold onto\add*, and gave them to the Assyrian king.
\p
\v 17 However, the Assyrian king \add still\add* sent his general and some of his top officials from Lakish to King Hezekiah in Jerusalem. They arrived at Jerusalem with a large army and camped by the aquifer supplying the upper pool that was near the field where the people washed their clothes.
\v 18 They called out to the king, and Hilkiyah’s son Eliakim who was the palace manager, and the scribe Shevna, and Asaf’s son Yoah the secretary, went out to them.
\p
\v 19 Then the top \add Assyrian\add* commander said to them, “Now, tell Hezekiah that the great Assyrian king asks him who he thinks he’s trusting in.
\v 20 He claims to be powerful enough to fight us. Who is he trusting to help you all \add that gives you confidence\add* to rebel against us?
\v 21 Listen, your king’s trusting in a broken stick to lean on which will just splinter and pierce his hand. That’s what King Pharaoh of Egypt is like to everyone who puts their trust in him.
\v 22 \add Ah,\add* but he might tell me that he’s trusting in your god Yahweh to help you all. If so, I’d ask him if he isn’t the one whose hilltop shrines \add King\add* Hezekiah demolished when he told you people in Jerusalem and all Judah that you have to worship at the altar there?
\p
\v 23 “So now ask your king if he’ll make a deal with my master, the king of Assyria: He’ll give you two thousand horses, on the condition that you can supply two thousand horsemen who can ride them.
\v 24 \add If you can’t do that,\add* how could you all possibly repel even one of our army units? \add Haha,\add* but \add of course\add* you trust in Egypt \add to supply\add* chariots and horsemen.
\v 25 Do you think that we’ve come here to destroy this place without Yahweh’s permission? \add No, no,\add* it was Yahweh himself who told us to attack and destroy you.”
\p
\v 26 But Eliakim and Shevna and Yoah asked the Assyrian commander, “Please speak Syrian to your servants because we understand it. Don’t speak our language because our people on the nearby city wall will be able to understand it.”
\p
\v 27 “Ha ha, do you think my master sent this message just to you three and your king?” he replied. “\add No,\add* don’t you think that this message is also for the hungry people sitting on the wall who’ll soon have to eat their own dung and drink their own urine along with you?”
\p
\v 28 Then he stood up and called out loudly in Hebrew, “\add Everyone\add* listen to what the great king from Assyria says:
\v 29 He’s warning you all not to let Hezekiah deceive you, because he’s unable to save you all from our army.
\v 30 And don’t let him force you all to trust in Yahweh thinking that we won’t capture your city and that Yahweh will somehow rescue you all.
\v 31 Don’t listen to Hezekiah because the Assyrian king is offering you all a chance to come out of the city and surrender. In exchange for saving me some trouble, you’ll be able to drink fresh water again and enjoy the fruit off your own trees \add out here\add*
\v 32 until he comes here. Then he’ll take you to another country like your own—with grain and wine, and bread and vineyards, olive oil and honey. That way you’ll live and not die \add of starvation\add*. So don’t listen to Hezekiah when he misleads you saying that Yahweh will rescue you all.”
\v 33 Did the gods of \add any of\add* the \add other\add* countries rescue their people from the power of the Assyrian king?
\v 34 Where were the gods of Hamat and Arpad? Where were the gods of Sefarvayim, Hena, and Ivah? Were they able to save Samaria from the king’s power?
\v 35 From all the other countries, which of their gods was able to save their people, that might give confidence that Yahweh might be able to rescue Jerusalem from the king’s power?”
\p
\v 36 But the people \add on the wall listening\add* remained silent—they didn’t say a word because the king had already ordered them not to answer the Assyrians.
\v 37 Then Hilkiyah’s son Eliakim the palace manager, Shevna the scribe and Asaf’s son Yoah the secretary went back in the city to Hezekiah, tearing their clothes as they went, and they relayed the words of the chief commander to him.
\c 19
\s1 Hezekiah consults Isaiah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (Isa. 37:1-7)
\p
\v 1 When King Hezekiah heard \add the threats from the Assyrian king\add*, he tore his clothes and dressed in \add coarse, abrasive\add* sackcloth, and went into Yahweh’s temple.
\v 2 He sent his palace manager Eliakim and the scribe Shevna, along with the elders of the priests, \add all\add* dressed in sackcloth, to Amots’s son Isaiah the prophet
\v 3 to tell him, “Hezekiah says: ‘Today is a day of distress and rebuke and disgrace, as if the baby is right there ready to be delivered, but the mother has lost all her strength.
\v 4 Perhaps your god Yahweh has heard everything that the chief commander said when his master the Assyrian king sent him: he defied the living God, and so maybe your god Yahweh will punish him for his words. Lift up a prayer on behalf of the people that are still left here.’ ”
\p
\v 5 When King Hezekiah’s servants got to Isaiah,
\v 6 he told them, “This is what you all should tell your master: Yahweh says that you needn’t be afraid of what you heard when those young men from the Assyrian king insulted him.
\v 7 Listen, Yahweh will cause him to hear a report and he’ll place a \add fearful\add* spirit in him so \add he’ll decide to\add* return to his own country where he’ll be assassinated.”
\s1 The Assyrians threaten again
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (Isa. 37:8-20)
\p
\v 8 When the chief commander returned to the Assyrian king, he discovered that they’d pulled out of Lakish and were now fighting against Livnah \add city\add*.
\v 9 Then the king heard that the Ethiopian King Tirhakah was preparing to attack, so he \add decided to\add* return \add home\add* but he sent messengers to Hezekiah to say,
\v 10 “Tell Judah’s King Hezekiah not to let the god he trusts in deceive him by telling him that Jerusalem won’t be captured by the king of Assyria.
\v 11 Tell him that he must have heard how the Assyrian armies have completely devastated other countries, so he shouldn’t think that he will be saved from it.
\v 12 The gods of the countries destroyed by my ancestors never saved them—those in Gozan, Haran, Retsef, or Eden’s descendants in Telasar.
\v 13 Where’s the king of Hamat, or the king of Arpad, or the kings of the cities of Sefarvayim, Hena, or Ivah \add now\add*?”
\p
\v 14 Hezekiah took the letter that the messengers had brought and read it, then he went up to the temple and spread it out in front of Yahweh
\v 15 and prayed to him, “Yahweh the god of Israel, who lives above the winged creatures. You alone are God—the one over all the kingdoms of the earth. You yourself made the heavens and the earth.
\v 16 Lean this way, Yahweh, and look, and listen to Sennacherib’s words mocking the living God.
\v 17 \add Yes\add* Yahweh, the Assyrian kings have certainly destroyed many countries and their lands.
\v 18 The Assyrians burnt the peoples’ gods because they weren’t \add living\add* gods, but rather \add gods\add* of wood and stone made by people and they’ve destroyed them.
\v 19 But now Yahweh our god, please save us from his army, then all the kingdoms in the world will know that you, Yahweh, are God—you alone.”
\s1 Yahweh’s response to Hezekiah via Isaiah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (Isa. 37:21-38)
\p
\v 20 Then Amots’s son Isaiah sent this message to Hezekiah: Israel’s god Yahweh says, “Because you prayed to me concerning the Assyrian King Sennacherib, I have listened.
\v 21 This is what Yahweh says about that king:
\q1 Zion’s daughter despises you and derides you.
\q1 Jerusalem’s daughter shakes her head at you.
\q1
\v 22 Who \add did you think\add* you were teasing and insulting?
\q1 Who \add did you think\add* you were shouting at?
\q1 Did you raise your eyebrows against Israel’s holy one?
\q1
\v 23 You sent messengers that mocked me.
\q1 You said that you went over the highest mountains with your many chariots.
\q1 That you went to the highest parts of Lebanon and harvested its tallest cedars—its best trees.
\q1 That you’ve been to the end of the inhabited world with its densest forest.
\q1
\v 24 You said that you’ve dug wells far away and drunk their water,
\q1 yet with your own feet you dried up all of Egypt’s rivers.
\q1
\v 25 Haven’t you heard that I made plans long ago—
\q1 that what I previously planned, I’m now making it happen?
\q1 Fortified cities will collapse into heaps of rubble.
\q1
\v 26 Their inhabitants will be powerless—dismayed and ashamed.
\q1 They’ll be \add as vulnerable\add* as plants in the countryside,
\q1 or like the grass that grows on the rooftops—
\q1 they wilt and wither before they can grow tall. DOUBLE-CHECK
\q1
\v 27 \add Yes,\add* I know when you sit down and when you go out.
\q1 When you come in and rage against me.
\q1
\v 28 Because you’ve raged against me and your arrogance has come to my ears,
\q1 I’ll put my hook in your nose and my bit in your mouth,
\q1 and I’ll lead you back on the road that you came here on.
\p
\v 29 “So this will be a sign to you \add Hezekiah\add*:
\q1 This year you’ll eat what grew by itself,
\q1 and next year whatever seeded by itself,
\q1 ^but in the third year you’ll sow \add crops\add* and plant vineyards, and eat what you harvest.
\q1
\v 30 Judah’s surviving descendants will send their roots downwards and will produce fruit above,
\q1
\v 31 because a remnant will survive Jerusalem’s siege,
\q1 and Mt. Zion will have survivors
\q1 because Yahweh’s enthusiasm will make sure it happens.
\p
\v 32 “So this is what Yahweh says to the Assyrian king:
\q1 He won’t enter this city or shoot an arrow into it.
\q1 He won’t push a large shield towards it or make a ramp up into it.
\q1
\v 33 He’ll return on the same road that he arrived on,
\q1 and Yahweh declares that he won’t enter this city,
\q1
\v 34 because Yahweh will defend this city
\q1 to save it for his own sake
\q2 and for the sake of his servant David.”
\p
\v 35 That very night, Yahweh \add sent\add* an angel out to kill 185,000 \add warriors, so when the army\add* got up early the next morning there were dead bodies all over the place.
\v 36 So the Assyrian King Sennacherib pulled out and went back to live in Nineveh.
\v 37 While he was bowing in the temple of his god Nisrok, Adramelek and Sharetser ran him through with a sword before escaping to the Ararat region, and \add so\add* his son Esar-Hadon replaced him as king.
\c 20
\s1 Hezekiah’s sickness and recovery
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (Isa. 38:1-8,21-22; 2 Chr. 32:24-26)
\p
\v 1 By that time, \add King\add* Hezekiah was terminally ill and Amots’s son the prophet Isaiah came to him and told him, “Yahweh says to get your affairs in order because you’re dying and won’t recover.”
\p
\v 2 But Hezekiah \add rolled over\add* to face the wall and prayed to Yahweh,
\v 3 “Oh Yahweh, please remember that I’ve served you faithfully, and done what you asked with total sincerity.” Then Hezekiah cried loudly.
\p
\v 4 As Isaiah was leaving, Yahweh gave him this message before he’d even reached the middle courtyard,
\v 5 “Go back and tell Hezekiah, the leader of my people, ‘Your ancestor David’s god Yahweh says that he’s heard your prayer \add and\add* seen your tears. He’s decided to heal you and you’ll be well enough to go to the temple within three days.
\v 6 He’s added fifteen years to your life, plus he’ll rescue you and this city from the Assyrian king. Yahweh will defend Jerusalem for his own sake and for the sake of his servant David.”
\p
\v 7 Then Isaiah told them to bring some pressed figs, and they brought them and placed them on the sore, and Hezekiah started getting better.
\p
\v 8 Hezekiah asked Isaiah, “What’s the sign that Yahweh will heal me and that I’ll be able to go to the temple on the third day?”
\p
\v 9 “\add Yes,\add* Yahweh will give you a sign that he’ll do what he said,” Isaiah replied. “Do you want the shadows to advance suddenly or go back?”
\p
\v 10 “It’s easy for the shadows to go forward,” said Hezekiah. “So make them go backwards ten steps.”
\p
\v 11 So the prophet Isaiah called to Yahweh, and he made the shadow go back on the steps \add made by King\add* Ahaz—the shadow went ten steps backward.
\s1 Hezekiah shows off his treasures
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (Isa. 39:1-8)
\p
\v 12 At that time, Baladan’s son \add former\add* King Merodak-Bal’adan of Babylon heard that Hezekiah was sick and sent him letters and a gift.
\v 13 \add When the messengers arrived,\add* Hezekiah listened to them, then he showed them his entire treasure house: the gold and silver, the spices and the best oil, the house of his armour, and everything that was in his treasuries. He didn’t keep anything in his palace or in his kingdom secret from them.
\v 14 Later the prophet Isaiah came to King Hezekiah and he asked him, “Where were those men from and what did they say?”
\p “They came from a distant land—from Babylon,” he replied.
\p
\v 15 “What did they see in your house?” Isaiah asked.
\p “They saw everything that’s in my house,” he replied. “There wasn’t anything in my treasuries that I didn’t show them.”
\p
\v 16 Then Isaiah told Hezekiah, “Listen to Yahweh’s message:
\v 17 He says that days are coming when everything in your house, and everything that your ancestors stored \add carefully\add* away until this day will be carried to Babylon. Nothing will be left behind.
\v 18 \add What’s more,\add* some of your own biological descendants will be taken and they’ll be castrated to become servants of the Babylonian king.”
\p
\v 19 “What you said from Yahweh is fine,” Hezekiah replied to Isaiah. “At least I might have peace and stability in my time.”
\s1 Hezekiah’s death
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 32:32-33)
\p
\v 20 Everything else that Hezekiah said and did, including his making the pool and tunnel to bring water into the city, is written in the book of the events of the kings of Judah.
\v 21 In due course, Hezekiah died and his son Manasseh replaced him as king.
\c 21
\s1 Manasseh’s reign over Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 33:1-20)
\p
\v 1 Manasseh was twelve when he became king, and he reigned \add over Judah\add* from Jerusalem for fifty-five years. (His mother was Heftsi-Bah.)
\v 2 He did what Yahweh had said was evil like the evil behaviour of the people groups that Yahweh had driven out \add of the region\add* ahead of the Israelites.
\v 3 \add In fact\add* he went backwards from his father and rebuilt the hilltop shrines that Hezekiah had destroyed, and he made altars for Baal, and he made an Asherah pole, just like Israel’s King Ahab had, and he worshipped the constellations and followed astrology.
\v 4 Also he built altars in Yahweh’s residence, where Yahweh had said, “I will establish my reputation in Jerusalem.”
\v 5 Manasseh built altars to all the constellations in the two temple courtyards .
\v 6 He even sacrificed his own son as a burnt offering. He practised divination and read omens, and consulted mediums and fortune-tellers. He did so much that Yahweh had said was evil, provoking Yahweh to get angry.
\v 7 He placed the Asherah idol that he’d made in the temple about which Yahweh had said to David and to his son Solomon, “I will establish my reputation forever in this house and in Jerusalem \add in Judah\add*, which I’ve chosen out of all the tribes of Israel.
\v 8 If only the Israelites will take care to obey everything that I’ve commanded them, and all the instructions that my servant Moses gave them, then I won’t continue to cause them to leave the area that I gave to their ancestors.”
\v 9 But they didn’t listen and Manasseh led them astray to do more evil things \add even\add* than the people groups that Yahweh had destroyed as the people had entered the region.
\p
\v 10 So Yahweh announced via his servants the prophets,
\v 11 “Because Judah’s King Manasseh has done such terribly evil things, worse even than the Amorites who were there before him, and because he’s even made Judah sin with his idols,
\v 12 then Israel’s god Yahweh says: Listen, I’ll bring such terrible disaster to Judah including Jerusalem that people won’t even be able to handle hearing the news about it.
\v 13 I’ll use the same tape measure for Jerusalem that I used for Samaria, and I’ll use the same plumb line that I used for Ahab’s extended family. I’ll wipe Jerusalem away, just like someone dries a bowl—they wipe \add it dry\add* and then turn it upside down.
\v 14 I’ll abandon the small remainder of my people here, and I’ll hand them over to their enemies—they’ll become the spoil and plunder of all their enemies,
\v 15 because they’ve done what I told them was evil, and they’ve been making me angry from the time that their ancestors were brought out of Egypt right up to this day.”
\p
\v 16 Manasseh even poured out so much innocent blood that he filled Jerusalem \add with it\add* from one side to the other, and that was on top of his other sin of leading Judah into doing what Yahweh had said was evil.
\p
\v 17 Everything else that Manasseh said and did, including the sins that he committed, is written in the book of the events of the kings of Judah.
\v 18 Then Manasseh died and was buried in the palace garden (made by Uza), and his son Amon replaced him as king.
\s1 Amon’s reign over Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 19 Amon was twenty-two when he became king, and he reigned from Jerusalem for two years. (His mother was Haruts’s daughter Meshulemet from Yotvah.)
\v 20 He did what Yahweh had said was evil, just like his father Manasseh had done.
\v 21 \add In fact,\add* he followed everything that his father had done, \add including\add* serving the same idols and bowing to them.
\v 22 He abandoned Yahweh, the god of his ancestors, and didn’t obey his instructions.
\p
\v 23 But Amon’s servants plotted to assassinate him, and they killed him in the palace.
\v 24 However, the people executed everyone who’d been involved in the conspiracy against King Amon, and they installed his son Josiah as king in his place.
\p
\v 25 Everything else that Amon said and did is written in the book of the events of the kings of Judah.
\v 26 He was buried in his tomb in the garden \add made by\add* Uza, and his son Josiah replaced him as king.
\c 22
\s1 Josiah’s reign over Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 34:1-2)
\p
\v 1 Josiah was eight when he became king, and he reigned from Jerusalem for thirty-one years. (His mother was Adayah’s daughter Yedidah from Batsekat.)
\v 2 He did what Yahweh had said was correct behaviour, following all the customs of his ancestor \add King\add* David, and obeying Yahweh’s instructions.
\s1 A scroll is discovered in the temple
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 34:8-28)
\p
\v 3 In the eighth month of the eighteenth year of King Josiah’s reign, he sent the scribe Shafan (the son of Atsalyah the son of Meshulam) to the temple, saying
\v 4 “Go to the high priest Hilkiyah and get him to count the silver that was brought into Yahweh’s temple, which the door-keepers collected from the people.
\v 5 Then have it given to the supervisors of the temple repairs, and then they can pay the workers who are repairing the damage—
\v 6 the craftsmen, builders, and masons, as well as buying wood and quarried stones for the repairs.”
\v 7 They won’t need to submit detailed accounts for it because they’re trustworthy people.
\p
\v 8 \add During the repairs,\add* the high priest Hilkiyah told the scribe Shafan, “I found a scroll in the temple with Yahweh’s instructions written on it.” So Hilkiyah gave the scroll to Shafan to read.
\v 9 Then Shafan the scribe went to the king with this report, “Your servants handed over the money that had been collected in the temple, and they gave it to the supervisors of the workers doing the repairs.”
\v 10 Then he added, “And the priest Hilkiyah gave me a scroll.” Then he read it \add out loud\add* to King \add Josiah\add*.
\p
\v 11 When the king heard the contents of the scroll, he tore his clothes
\v 12 and commanded the priest Hilkiyah, Shafan’s son Ahikam, Mikayah’s son Akbor, the scribe Shafan, and the king’s servant Asayah,
\v 13 “Go \add and\add* inquire from Yahweh on my behalf and on behalf of the people and all Judah, concerning the words of this scroll that was found. Because \add it sounds like\add* Yahweh must be very angry at us because our predecessors didn’t listen to what’s written on this scroll and didn’t do what was expected of us.”
\p
\v 14 So Hilkiyah the priest and Ahikam, Akbor, Shafan, and Asayah went to the prophetess Huldah (the wife of Shalum, the son of Tikvah, the son of Harhas, the one in charge of looking after the priests’ uniforms who lived in the newer part of Jerusalem, and they spoke to her.
\v 15 She told them that Israel’s god Yahweh had said, “Tell the man who sent you all to me
\v 16 that Yahweh says this: I’m going to bring disaster to this place and its inhabitants, just like it’s written in the scroll that the king read
\v 17 because they abandoned me. They offered sacrifices to other gods in order to make me angry at everything they do, so now my anger will be directed against this place and it’s not stoppable.
\v 18 But to the king of Judah who sent you all to seek Yahweh, tell him that Israel’s god Yahweh says: The words that you heard \add from the scroll\add*,
\v 19 because you’re open to learn and because you humbled yourself in front of Yahweh when you heard my promise that this place and its inhabitants would become a horror and a curse, and because you’ve torn your clothes and wept in front of me, then Yahweh said that he’s taken notice of you.
\v 20 Because of that, he’ll allow you to die and be buried peacefully, and you yourself won’t witness the destruction that will come to this place.”
\p So they relayed those messages back to King \add Josiah\add*.
\c 23
\s1 Josiah removes false gods
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 34:3-7,29-33)
\p
\v 1 Then King \add Josiah\add* summoned all the elders of Jerusalem and \add across\add* Judah,
\v 2 and he went to the temple, and all the inhabitants of Jerusalem and from all across Judah went with him, along with the priests and prophets, and all the people from the most to the least important. Then he read to them every word on the scroll of the agreement that had been found in Yahweh’s residence.
\v 3 Then the king stood by the pillar and he made a commitment in front of Yahweh to follow Yahweh and to obey his commands and testimonies and statutes with all sincerity, and with every desire to respect the words of that agreement that had been written on that scroll. All the people were also included in that commitment.
\p
\v 4 Then the king commanded the high priest Hilkiyah and the other priests, and the temple guards to bring out all the utensils that were made for Baal and Asherah and for all the constellations, and he burnt them outside Jerusalem in the Kidron countryside, and carried their ashes to Bethel.
\v 5 He got rid of the pagan priests that the kings of Judah had appointed to burn incense in the hilltop shrines around Jerusalem and across \add the rest of\add* Judah, and the ones who burnt incense to Baal, and to the sun and moon and to the planets and constellations.
\v 6 He got the Asherah pole out from Yahweh’s temple and burnt it in the Kidron valley outside Jerusalem, then he pounded the ashes to dust and threw it over people’s graves.
\v 7 He demolished the cubicles of the male temple prostitutes that were inside Yahweh’s temple where the women were weaving Asherah coverings.\f + \fr 23:7 \ft It’s not exactly clear what these woven objects were, or how or where they were used.\f*
\v 8 He brought all the priests \add to Jerusalem\add* from the cities of Judah, and he desecrated the hilltop shrines where the priests had burnt incense, from Geva to Beersheba. He demolished the hilltop shrines that Joshua a city official) had built near the city gate (on the left of the gate as you entered the city).
\v 9 However those priests from those hilltop shrines weren’t allowed to serve at the altar in Jerusalem, but they were allowed to eat unleavened bread like the other priests.
\p
\v 10 Josiah \add also\add* desecrated the Tofet, which was in the Ben-Hinom valley, so that the people couldn’t sacrifice their children to Molech.
\v 11 The horses that the kings of Judah had offered to the sun, he prevented from approaching the hall of the official Netan-Melek that was in the courtyards of Yahweh’s temple, and he set fire to the chariots of the sun.
\v 12 Then the king tore down the altars that were on the roof in Ahaz’s upper chamber that the kings of Judah made, and the altars that Manasseh made in the two courtyards of Yahweh’s temple and he threw the rubble into the Kidron valley.
\v 13 The king desecrated the hilltop shrines that faced Jerusalem—they were south of the Mt. of Destruction’ \add (the Mt. of Olives)\add*. King Solomon had built shrines for Ashtarot (the disgusting \add god\add* of the Sidonians and for Chemosh (the disgusting \add god\add* of Moab and for Molech (the disgusting \add god\add* of the Ammonites).
\v 14 He \add also\add* smashed the \add sacred\add* pillars and cut down the Asherah poles, then he \add desecrated\add* those places by filling them with human bones.
\p
\v 15 He also demolished the altar that was in Bethel—the hilltop shrine that Nevat’s son Jeroboam had made when he’d caused Israel to sin. He demolished both that altar and the hilltop shrine, then he burnt the shrine. He crushed everything to dust, and he burnt the Asherah pole.
\v 16 As Josiah turned \add around\add*, he noticed some graves that were there on the hillside, so he had some bones removed from the graves, and he burnt them on the altar to desecrate it. That fulfilled what Yahweh had said through the man of God who’d declared those things.
\v 17 Josiah \add turned again and\add* asked, “Whose tomb is that?”
\p “It’s the prophet’s tomb,” the people of Bethel replied, “\add The one\add* who came from Judah and predicted that what you just did to that altar would happen.”
\v 18 “Don’t disturb it then,” the king ordered. “Don’t let anyone remove his bones.”
\p So they left his bones alone, \add along with\add* the bones of the \add other\add* prophet who came from Samaria.
\v 19 So \add King\add* Josiah removed all the hilltop shrines that the Israelite kings had made in the cities of Samaria, provoking \add Yahweh’s\add* anger. The king destroyed them just like he’d done to the ones in Bethel.
\v 20 He executed all the priests from those hilltop shrines on the altars there, and then he burnt human bones on them \add to desecrate them\add*. Then he returned to Jerusalem.
\s1 The celebration of ‘pass-over’
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 35:1-19)
\p
\v 21 Then the king commanded all the people, “Celebrate ‘pass-over’ for your god Yahweh according to what’s written on this scroll with the agreement.”
\v 22 Since the days of the leaders who judged Israel, or in all the days of the kings of Israel and Judah, a ‘pass-over’ had never been celebrated like that one
\v 23 in the eighteenth year of King Josiah’s \add reign\add*. That ‘pass-over’ \add celebration\add* in Jerusalem was \add to honour\add* Yahweh.
\s1 King Josiah’s other activities
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 24 \add King\add* Josiah also removed the ritual pits and the soothsayers, and the images and idols, and all the abhorrences that were seen in Jerusalem and in the Judah region, in his diligence to obey everything that was written on the scroll that the priest Hilkiyah found in the temple.
\v 25 No other king had been like Josiah who’d led the people back to obeying Yahweh—following Moses’ written instructions with all his heart and all his spirit and all his energy. \add What’s more,\add* there’s never been \add a king\add* like him since then either.
\p
\v 26 However, Yahweh’s anger against Judah hadn’t cooled down after everything that King Manasseh had done to make him angry,
\v 27 and Yahweh said, “I will also remove Judah out of my sight, just like I removed Israel. I’ll also reject this city of Jerusalem that I chose, as well as the house where I’d said that my name would be established.”
\p
\v 28 Everything else that Josiah said and did is written in the book of the events of the kings of Judah.
\v 29 While he was still king, the Egyptian king Pharaoh Nekoh attacked the Assyrian king near the Euphrates river. King Josiah went to meet him, but Nekoh killed him at Megiddo when he saw him.
\v 30 His servants brought his body back to Jerusalem from Megiddo, and they buried him in his own tomb. Then the people got his son Yehoahaz, and they anointed him and made him king in place of his father.
\s1 Yehoahaz’s reign over Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 36:2-4)
\p
\v 31 Yehoahaz was twenty-three when he became king, and he \add reigned\add* from Jerusalem for \add only\add* three months. (His mother was Jeremiah’s daughter Hamutal from Livnah.)
\v 32 He did what Yahweh had said was evil like \add many of\add* his ancestors had done.
\v 33 Pharaoh Nekoh took him from Jerusalem and imprisoned him at Rivlah in the Hamat region, and he forced the residents of Judah to pay him thirty kilograms of gold and three tonnes of silver.
\v 34 Then Nekoh appointed Josiah’s son Eliakim as the new king but changed his name to Yehoyakim. Then he took Yehoahaz to Egypt where he eventually died.
\v 35 So Yehoyakim paid the gold and silver to Pharaoh by assessing the wealth of the people and then forcibly collecting the gold and silver from them to send to Pharaoh Nekoh.
\s1 Yehoyakim’s reign over Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 36:5-8)
\p
\v 36 Yehoyakim was twenty-five when he became king, and he \add reigned\add* from Jerusalem for eleven years. (His mother was Pedayah’s daughter Zevidah from Rumah.)
\v 37 He did what Yahweh had said was evil like \add many of\add* his ancestors had done.
\c 24
\p
\v 1 During his reign, the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar attacked, and Yehoyakim ruled under him for three years before rebelling against him.
\v 2 Then Yahweh sent troops of Chaldeans, troops from Syria, troops from Moab, and Ammonite troops against Judah \add at different times\add* to destroy them, just as Yahweh had said through his servants the prophets.
\v 3 Those things troubled Judah at Yahweh’s command to remove them out of his sight because of all of \add King\add* Manasseh’s sins
\v 4 and because he’d killed innocent people—Yahweh wouldn’t forgive him because he’d filled Jerusalem with innocent blood.
\p
\v 5 Everything else that Yehoyakim said and did is written in the book of the events of the kings of Judah.
\v 6 Then Yehoyakim died and his son Yehoyakin replaced him as king.
\p
\v 7 The Egyptian king didn’t continue his attacks on other countries, because the Babylonian king captured land all the way from the Egyptian river as far as the Euphrates river—everything that had been controlled by Egypt.
\s1 Yehoyakin’s reign over Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 36:9-10)
\p
\v 8 Yehoyakin was eighteen when he became king, but he reigned from Jerusalem for \add only\add* three months.
\v 9 He did what Yahweh had said was evil just like his father had done.
\p
\v 10 At that time, the Babylonian king Nebuchadnezzar sent his army to besiege Jerusalem,
\v 11 and Nebuchadnezzar himself travelled there to observe the operation.
\v 12 Judah’s King Yehoyakin went out \add to surrender\add* to the Babylonian king, along with his mother, his servants, his captains, and his officials. So the Babylonian king took him \add captive\add* in the eighth year of his reign.
\v 13 Then all the valuables were brought out of Yahweh’s temple and the palace. All the gold furnishings that had been made for the temple by King Solomon were cut into pieces.
\v 14 Then all the army leaders and officials, and all the craftsmen and blacksmiths from Jerusalem (some ten thousand people) were taken into exile, leaving only the poorer people behind.
\p
\v 15 King Yehoyakin was exiled from Jerusalem to Babylon, along with his mother and wives, his officials,
\v 16 seven thousand top soldiers, and a thousand skilled craftsmen and blacksmiths—leaving no one behind to fight or make weapons.
\p
\v 17 Then the Babylonian king appointed Yehoyakin’s uncle Matanyah as king in his place and he changed his name to Zedekiah.
\s1 Zedekiah’s reign over Judah
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (2 Chr. 36:11-12; Jer. 52:1-3a)
\p
\v 18 Zedekiah was twenty-one when he became king and he reigned from Jerusalem for eleven years. (His mother was Jeremiah’s daughter Hamutal from Livnah.)
\v 19 He did what Yahweh had said was evil, just like Yehoyakin had done.
\v 20 Because Yahweh was still very angry, he had the people of Jerusalem and all Judah driven away out of his sight.
\p Then Zedekiah rebelled against the Babylonian king.
\c 25
\s1 Jerusalem’s defeat
\s4 Southern kingdom
\p
\v 1 In the ninth year of Zedekiah’s reign, on the tenth day of the tenth month, the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar brought all his army to Jerusalem. They made their camp outside the city, and then built attack structures all around it
\v 2 and besieged the city for two years.
\v 3 The people didn’t have enough to eat and the famine became severe.
\v 4 Then the Babylonians began breaking into the city, but the local fighters sneaked out at night through a gate between two walls near the king’s garden and escaped \add down\add* to the desert plain.
\v 5 However, the Babylonian soldiers chased after the king and they overtook him on the Jericho plains, and his army scattered.
\v 6 King \add Zedekiah\add* was captured and taken to the Babylonian king at Rivlah, where he was sentenced—
\v 7 He was forced to watch as his sons were slaughtered, then his eyes were gouged out and he was taken to Babylon restrained with two bronze chains.
\s1 The demolition of the temple
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (Jer. 52:12-33)
\p
\v 8 On the seventh day of the fifth month of Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar’s nineteenth year as king, his servant Nevuzar-Adan, who was his chief bodyguard, went to Jerusalem.
\v 9 He set fire to Yahweh’s temple and the palace, and all of Jerusalem’s houses, so no important building remained.
\v 10 Then the army under the command of Nevuzar-Adan tore down the walls surrounding Jerusalem.
\v 11 He exiled all the rest of the people from the city, all the surrendered soldiers, and the rest of the population,
\v 12 but he let some of the poorest people remain on the land to look after the vineyards and as farmers.
\p
\v 13 The Babylonians smashed the bronze pillars and the bases and the bronze ‘sea’ from the temple, and took all the bronze to Babylon.
\v 14 They also took the pots and shovels, the snuffers and spoons, and all the bronze utensils used in the temple activities.
\v 15 They took the fire-pans and the gold and silver bowls.
\v 16 \add The bronze from\add* the two pillars, the ‘sea’, and the bases that had been made for the temple by Solomon was too heavy to be weighed.
\v 17 Each pillar was over eight metres high, plus a bronze capital on top that was over a metre high. They were decorated with latticework with bronze pomegranates all around.
\s1 The people of Judah get exiled to Babylon
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (Jer. 52:24-27)
\p
\v 18 Nevuzar-Adan exiled \add to Babylon\add* the high priest Serayah, the second priest Zephaniah, and the three \add temple\add* entrance guards.
\v 19 From the city, he took one official who was a military inspector, five of the king’s advisors, and the army commander’s secretary in charge of recruitment, plus sixty other important men.
\v 20 Nevuzar-Adan took them all to the Babylonian king at Rivlah
\v 21 in the Hamat region, but the king had them all executed there.
\s1 The governor there to Judah Gidaliyas
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (Jer. 40:7-9; 41:1-3)
\p So \add the large majority\add* of the people of Judah were exiled out of their country.
\v 22 From those who the Babylonian King Nebuchadnezzar allowed to remain, he appointed Gedalyah (son of Shafan’s son Ahikam) over them.
\v 23 When all the army captains and their men heard that the Babylonian king had appointed Gedalyah, they came to Gedalyah at Mitspah. \add This was\add* Netanyah’s son Ishmael, Kareah’s son Yohanan, Tanhumet’s son Serayah the Netofati, and the Ma’akati’s son Ya’azanyah, along with their men.
\v 24 Gedalyah made an agreement with them and their men, telling them, “Don’t be afraid of the Babylonian officials. Stay in the land and serve the Babylonian king, and he’ll be good to you.”
\v 25 But in the seventh month, Ishmael (the son of Netanyah, the son of Elishama who was a descendant of King David) brought ten men with him and attacked Gedalyah, and killed him, along with the Judeans and the Chaldeans who were with him at Mitspah.
\v 26 After that, all the people, with or without any official status, along with the army commanders fled to Egypt because they were afraid of what the Babylonians might do to them.
\s1 Yehoyakin gets released from prison
\s4 Southern kingdom
\r (Jer. 52:31-34)
\p
\v 27 Thirty-seven years after Judah’s King Yehoyakin had been exiled \add to Babylon\add*, Evil-Merodak had just become the new king of Babylon and he released Yehoyakin from prison on the 27th of the twelfth month.
\v 28 He spoke kindly to Yehoyakin and honoured him more than the other kings who’d been taken to Babylon.
\v 29 He was allowed to change out of his prison clothes, and was permitted to eat at the king’s table for the rest of his life,
\v 30 as well as being given a daily monetary allowance.
